00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:29.200
and in their own houses.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:30.970
In order to maintain social distancing

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:33.592
which you'll hear a lot
about, spoken about tonight.

00:00:33.592 --> 00:00:35.455
I'm gonna introduce our panelists.

00:00:35.455 --> 00:00:38.138
They're each gonna speak
for five minutes or so.

00:00:38.138 --> 00:00:41.300
Afterwards, we'll go to
a series of questions.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:45.130
We asked you to send in
questions until 4:30 p.m. today

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:47.680
and you sent in over 400 questions.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:50.190
So, we tried to pull a
representative sample

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:53.984
in order to give you a
wide breadth of answers

00:00:53.984 --> 00:00:56.282
to the things that you've
all been thinking about.

00:00:56.282 --> 00:00:58.710
So, without further ado
I'm just gonna introduce

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:01.573
our panelists who you'll
see on the screen here.

00:01:02.847 --> 00:01:05.270
I'm going to introduce them
in order of their appearance.

00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:08.260
First is Ellen Foxman who's
an Assistant Professor

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:11.431
of Laboratory Medicine and Immunobiology

00:01:11.431 --> 00:01:14.070
at the Yale School of Medicine.

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:15.920
She's going to talk about
what is Coronavirus?

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:16.753
Is it like the flu?

00:01:16.753 --> 00:01:17.630
What are its symptoms?

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:20.550
It's prognosis for different risk groups.

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:25.550
Sten Vermund is the Dean
and the Anna M.R. Lauder Professor

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:28.180
of Public Health at the Yale
School of Public Health.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:29.980
And he's gonna talk about
the possible scenarios

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:33.010
that could play out over the
next few weeks and months.

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:34.260
How bad might it get?

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.949
What are the population level expectations

00:01:36.949 --> 00:01:38.689
for this pandemic?

00:01:38.689 --> 00:01:41.683
Next, we'll hear from
Ann Kurth who's the Dean and the

00:01:43.046 --> 00:01:45.310
Linda Lorimer Professor at
the Yale School of Nursing

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:46.700
and she's gonna talk about what we can do

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:50.320
to protect ourselves, our
families and our communities.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.140
Next we'll here from Rajita Sinha,

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:55.420
who's a Foundation Funds
Professor of Psychiatry

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:56.380
at the Yale School of Medicine.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:57.840
And she's gonna talk about how we cope

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.060
with the changes in our lives.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.610
The dramatic one for being
asked to make right now.

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:05.360
Next we have Amy
Kapczynski, Professor at the

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:06.600
Yale School of Law.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.760
And she will talk about
what we can do to ensure

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:10.400
rights are being respected and the larger

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.463
social justice issues that
are raised by this pandemic.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.020
The last three speakers
are gonna talk about

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:17.070
what's happening locally.

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.470
Paul Genecin, who's the
Chief Executive Office

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.510
of Yale Health is gonna talk
about what is Yale doing.

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:23.491
What is Yale Health doing?

00:02:23.491 --> 00:02:26.170
Mehul Dalal who's a
Community Services Director

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:27.700
of the City of New Haven
is gonna talk about

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:29.482
what the City of New
Haven that we all live in

00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:30.880
is doing right now.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:33.213
And finally we have Sanil Parikh,

00:02:33.213 --> 00:02:35.480
who's an Associate
Professor of Epidemiology

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:36.460
at the Yale School of Public Health.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:39.040
Is going to talk about what
Yale New Haven Hospital,

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:40.661
our local hospital, is doing.

00:02:40.661 --> 00:02:43.020
So, I'm gonna ask Ellen to start us off.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:46.640
Then we'll go to Sten and Rajita, Amy,

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:51.640
Paul, Mehul and Sanil with
five minute of opening remarks.

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:54.020
We'll go directly in
order, one to the next.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:59.020
And then in about 45 minutes
we'll turn to questions, Ellen.

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:00.400
- Thank you Gregg.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:01.500
Well, hello everybody.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:04.660
It's nice to be with you
virtually as we're all

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:06.495
in our different locations.

00:03:06.495 --> 00:03:09.910
But I want to tell you a little bit about

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:11.817
this new Coronavirus and how it's similar

00:03:11.817 --> 00:03:15.410
and different to the viruses
that you're familiar with.

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:18.840
As you may know, viruses are
tiny germs that when they

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:22.160
get into your body they make
more copies of themselves.

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:25.570
And in the process of doing
that create collateral damage

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:27.048
which gives you symptoms.

00:03:27.048 --> 00:03:30.620
So, if that process is
happening in your nose

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:32.911
you might get a stuffy nose of runny nose.

00:03:32.911 --> 00:03:34.720
If that's happening in your throat

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:36.210
you might have a sore throat.

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:37.780
And if that's happening in your lungs

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:39.530
you might have shortness of breath.

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:42.820
Now, we have a lot of viruses
we're all familiar with

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:44.130
that do this every year.

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:48.166
Such as common cold virus, the flu virus.

00:03:48.166 --> 00:03:50.900
And we've all experienced these symptoms

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:52.163
to different degrees.

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:55.510
So, the question is what's different about

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:57.060
this new Coronavirus?

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:00.080
Why we should be taking
all these extra precautions

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:02.780
about it and be extra worried about it

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:05.760
and it comes down to this.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.771
The reason why this virus is so concerning

00:04:07.771 --> 00:04:10.980
is it's a new virus in
the human population.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:13.180
So, the other viruses we're familiar with

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:15.850
like common cold virus and flu.

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:19.114
Those come every year which means that

00:04:19.114 --> 00:04:21.903
our bodies that have seen the virus before

00:04:21.903 --> 00:04:26.460
have built some immune
defenses against those viruses.

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:29.620
And that means the
virus can spread as well

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:33.991
and don't cause as much illness
as they otherwise would.

00:04:33.991 --> 00:04:35.990
The thing about the novel Coronavirus is

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:39.220
it wasn't in the human population before.

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:41.460
It recently came into humans just

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:43.476
a few months ago from another animal.

00:04:43.476 --> 00:04:47.220
And a few months ago there's nobody

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:50.070
in the human population who had built up

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:52.420
immune defenses against this virus.

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:56.490
So, what that means it that the virus

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:59.400
can spread more easily
from person to person.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:02.780
And also has potential to
cause more serious illness

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:06.150
in a given person then it
would have if it was a virus

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:09.050
that many individuals
had experienced before

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:11.273
and had built up defenses against.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.850
So, again just like the
common cold and the flu.

00:05:16.850 --> 00:05:18.810
It can infect the same parts of the body

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:21.145
in the airway and in the lungs.

00:05:21.145 --> 00:05:24.280
But unlike those viruses
it has a lot more potential

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:27.850
to spread from person to person and cause

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:30.700
serious illness in certain populations.

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:33.350
And just to answer the question.

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:35.760
The other question that Gregg posed.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:39.800
Those populations appear
to be people of older age.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:43.650
So, people over the age of
70 have been hardest hit

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:46.163
in getting severe symptoms by this virus.

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:50.429
Younger people, especially
people under the age of 20

00:05:50.429 --> 00:05:53.200
can still get the virus
and transmit the virus

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.343
but don't appear to have
very serious symptoms.

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:03.350
In general, another important factor is

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:06.510
if you're somebody who has
other long term health condition

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:10.250
such as breathing problems or diabetes,

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:11.083
those kinds of things.

00:06:11.083 --> 00:06:14.810
It's important to be extra
cautious with this virus.

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:17.050
- That's Ellen Foxman talking about

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:19.570
the pathogenesis of Coronavirus infection.

00:06:19.570 --> 00:06:21.490
We're gonna move to Sten
Vermund who's gonna talk about

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:24.570
the populations level
impact of the disease.

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:26.090
Sten, are you there?

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:27.563
- Thank you very much Gregg.

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:37.160
The phenomenon of influenza
every year is something that

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:41.085
washes over the globe quite predictably.

00:06:41.085 --> 00:06:46.085
And is a severe burden on the human race.

00:06:48.710 --> 00:06:52.240
We have a high degree of mutation.

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:56.270
So, there tends to be
a new strain each year.

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:59.150
And therefore, we have to have new

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:00.830
vaccine development each year.

00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:04.120
Every year we're guessing
what the new strain will be

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:05.270
in the subsequent year.

00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:11.470
This is why we are very keen
for people to be vaccinated

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:13.950
against influenza every year.

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:16.830
To reduce the global burden of infection

00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:21.363
and also of disease and death.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:26.210
The death rate for flu is
about one in a thousand.

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:28.890
And the death rate for the new Coronavirus

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:31.540
which seems to be about
as infectious as flu

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:33.940
as Professor Foxman just said.

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:37.610
The new Coronavirus, the death rate

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:39.810
may be one in a hundred.

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:42.563
So, 10 times greater than influenza.

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:47.237
Since this has been introduced to mankind

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:52.028
or humankind recently.

00:07:52.028 --> 00:07:56.999
We would be very pleased
to rid it from humankind

00:07:56.999 --> 00:08:01.650
the way that the 2004 original SARS

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:04.779
Coronavirus was eliminated.

00:08:04.779 --> 00:08:08.280
And we are not sure how to do that because

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:11.710
this particular Coronavirus
is more infectious

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:16.710
than that 2003, 2004
version of Coronavirus was.

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:21.680
But it's worth a try
because we cannot afford

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:26.514
to add another flu like respiratory virus

00:08:26.514 --> 00:08:31.514
that recurs and plaques
humankind year after year.

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:35.940
It is worth a try to see
if we can suppress it

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:41.520
sufficiently to reduce the
burden on our hospitals.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:46.210
To reduce the death rates in
our vulnerable populations,

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:48.370
mostly the elderly.

00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:53.108
And persons who have
underlying medical conditions.

00:08:53.108 --> 00:08:56.600
And it's critical really that we do this

00:08:56.600 --> 00:09:01.140
with a sense of urgency
because if we fail to

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:07.260
reduce viral transmission adequately.

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:12.260
When the new winter respiratory
pathogen season comes about

00:09:13.750 --> 00:09:16.470
we could see Coronavirus
added to influenza

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:19.100
as a major global threat.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:22.750
So, not only do we want to
so called flatten the curve

00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:26.150
to reduce the pace at which our hospitals

00:09:26.150 --> 00:09:29.533
are faced with this
very serious condition.

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:33.481
Not only do we want to reduce mortality.

00:09:33.481 --> 00:09:38.481
But we also want to
see if we can eliminate

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:42.908
this virus in this transmission season.

00:09:42.908 --> 00:09:46.980
And if we can do that it
may be that the summer time

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:51.920
is our friend with warmer
and more moist air.

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:56.250
We don't know if that will be
enough to get rid of the virus

00:09:56.250 --> 00:10:00.120
because it's possible that
herd immunity plays a role

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:04.430
in why respiratory viruses are seasonal.

00:10:04.430 --> 00:10:06.770
And if we have very little heard immunity

00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:09.334
perhaps that will work against us.

00:10:09.334 --> 00:10:11.320
But we're in unchartered territory.

00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:15.520
We've never confronted a
pandemic threat of this nature.

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:20.520
Unless you go back to the Spanish
influenza of 1918 to 1919.

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:25.893
So, at this point we
see a fairly rapid rise.

00:10:27.929 --> 00:10:30.786
The epidemic curve in the United States.

00:10:30.786 --> 00:10:35.786
It resembles China and Italy and Korea

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:41.610
more than it resembles Hong Kong

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:43.330
and Singapore for example.

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:47.470
Where they were successful
at containing viral spread.

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:52.470
So, we don't seem to have
gotten a handle on this yet.

00:10:52.605 --> 00:10:55.480
For example, there were a
thousand new cases reported

00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:59.290
from New York City just
in the past 24 hours.

00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:04.270
So, we are seeing the uptick
in the Connecticut curve

00:11:04.270 --> 00:11:07.390
as well as the uptick in curves in many

00:11:07.390 --> 00:11:10.443
countries and states.

00:11:10.443 --> 00:11:15.443
And we're going to have to
practice social distancing.

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:18.540
Shutdown our economy for a while.

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:23.043
See if we can control this
virus for the long term benefit.

00:11:26.770 --> 00:11:28.383
- Thanks Sten.

00:11:28.383 --> 00:11:30.350
Our next speaker is Ann Kurth who's

00:11:30.350 --> 00:11:32.620
the Dean of the Yale School
of Nursing as I said.

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:34.720
She's gonna talk about what we
can do to protect ourselves,

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:36.670
our families and our communities, Anne.

00:11:37.539 --> 00:11:39.770
- Great, thank you for joining.

00:11:39.770 --> 00:11:41.940
You're taking the time
to educate yourself.

00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:44.880
To plug into a community of
people who are also thinking

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:47.160
and working on this current crisis.

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:49.950
And so, I applaud you for doing that.

00:11:49.950 --> 00:11:52.190
And I just want to acknowledge
that this is in fact

00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:54.720
extremely stressful for everyone.

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:57.999
We've emphasized the fact
that this is a new pathogen.

00:11:57.999 --> 00:12:00.970
It's having an effect on our economies

00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:04.520
and our emotions in a way that
feels almost unprecedented.

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:07.580
And so, I think we can go
ahead and acknowledge that.

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:10.440
And think about the things
that we actually can do.

00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:13.290
So, what are some of the
things that we can control?

00:12:13.290 --> 00:12:16.160
And there are some common sense guidances

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:19.320
that are things that
you can do for yourself

00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:21.580
to reduce risks and for your family.

00:12:21.580 --> 00:12:24.233
And that will help in
your community as well.

00:12:24.233 --> 00:12:26.520
I'd say for some of these things is just

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:28.910
to try to keep yourself in as good mental

00:12:28.910 --> 00:12:31.170
and physical shape as possible.

00:12:31.170 --> 00:12:34.190
One way to perhaps do
that is to try to shutdown

00:12:34.190 --> 00:12:37.040
some of the constant noise
and the borage of news.

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:39.110
So, find one trustable source, like the

00:12:39.110 --> 00:12:41.860
Centers for Disease Control,
that you can listen to

00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:43.330
maybe once or twice a day.

00:12:43.330 --> 00:12:46.010
Not every hour, not every 15 minutes.

00:12:46.010 --> 00:12:50.400
Just to control and stay up
to date with trustable news

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:52.578
and information and science.

00:12:52.578 --> 00:12:54.470
You've heard about social distancing.

00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:55.918
We're all trying to practice that now.

00:12:55.918 --> 00:12:59.520
I like to say we're all standing
together six feet apart.

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:02.010
Because that is really
the perimeter in which

00:13:02.010 --> 00:13:05.210
this pathogen is spread by droplets

00:13:05.210 --> 00:13:08.180
when someone sneezes or coughs falls.

00:13:08.180 --> 00:13:10.690
So, you want to keep that perimeter.

00:13:10.690 --> 00:13:14.330
We also want to ensure that we
are not gathering in groups.

00:13:14.330 --> 00:13:16.580
And there have been many
guidances around this.

00:13:16.580 --> 00:13:19.694
As you see businesses, places of the arts.

00:13:19.694 --> 00:13:22.626
Even hospitals restricting the
number of people who come in.

00:13:22.626 --> 00:13:25.010
All of this is towards that common goal

00:13:25.010 --> 00:13:29.160
that we're working for to
reduce the transmission

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:33.710
of this Coronavirus from
one person to another.

00:13:33.710 --> 00:13:36.750
Hand washing, everyone
should know it by heart.

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:38.940
You're singing Happy
Birthday twice in your head

00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:41.930
as you wash with soap
and water for 20 seconds

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:44.090
multiple times a day.

00:13:44.090 --> 00:13:46.050
This is gonna be good for
the health of everyone,

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:47.310
including our small children.

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:49.976
So, make sure that they are
also getting their hands washed

00:13:49.976 --> 00:13:51.970
and managing our sneezes and coughs.

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:56.480
Again, to reduce the respiratory
drop of transmission.

00:13:56.480 --> 00:14:00.030
Speaking with your
children or young people

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:01.530
that you're taking care of.

00:14:01.530 --> 00:14:04.380
You'll just want to give them
information that's factual.

00:14:04.380 --> 00:14:06.040
That it acknowledges their concerns

00:14:06.040 --> 00:14:07.780
and that's age appropriate.

00:14:07.780 --> 00:14:10.010
And again, keep your own trusted sources

00:14:10.010 --> 00:14:11.293
of communication going.

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:14.330
Getting ready for taking care of yourself

00:14:14.330 --> 00:14:16.790
and others at home is
something to think about.

00:14:16.790 --> 00:14:19.700
So, if we should all be
sort of monitoring ourselves

00:14:19.700 --> 00:14:24.700
and if we start to have a
fever, dry cough and fatigue.

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:28.220
Ideally we would not
bring that to a workplace

00:14:28.220 --> 00:14:31.370
or to a group setting
where we might encounter

00:14:31.370 --> 00:14:34.450
other people if, in fact, we are infected.

00:14:34.450 --> 00:14:37.730
We are concerned about some
of our folks more than others.

00:14:37.730 --> 00:14:39.870
Who are at higher risk
of more severe morbidity

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:42.210
and mortality with COVID-19.

00:14:42.210 --> 00:14:44.250
This includes people over age 70

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:46.013
and people who are immune compromised.

00:14:46.013 --> 00:14:51.013
Have heart and other conditions
who would be more at risk.

00:14:51.710 --> 00:14:53.560
So, we want to take care of, particularly,

00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:56.400
our more vulnerable
members of the population.

00:14:56.400 --> 00:14:58.830
We want to ensure that if we are working

00:14:58.830 --> 00:15:00.470
in the workplace as I said.

00:15:00.470 --> 00:15:02.590
That we hopefully are getting the message

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:03.810
that we can work from home.

00:15:03.810 --> 00:15:07.610
Or that we will continue
to have the opportunity

00:15:07.610 --> 00:15:12.610
for our pay and our
entitlements to keep coming.

00:15:13.090 --> 00:15:15.400
And the US government has been looking at

00:15:15.400 --> 00:15:18.868
injecting money into the
economy in order to do that.

00:15:18.868 --> 00:15:20.699
So that we don't, in the midst of having

00:15:20.699 --> 00:15:24.690
our economy shutdown and
having to be more home bound.

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:26.300
Really run into trouble for keeping

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:28.306
our own families going economically.

00:15:28.306 --> 00:15:30.670
So, we do also need to know our rights

00:15:30.670 --> 00:15:33.500
and advocate for those good policies.

00:15:33.500 --> 00:15:34.618
Care at home, again.

00:15:34.618 --> 00:15:36.920
Schools and daycares
are often shutting down.

00:15:36.920 --> 00:15:40.637
So, just be able to cover child care.

00:15:40.637 --> 00:15:43.133
We are looking into, for example here,

00:15:44.219 --> 00:15:46.461
ways to try to get childcare coverage

00:15:46.461 --> 00:15:48.057
for our health workers.

00:15:48.057 --> 00:15:50.490
But we do need to go
into the health system

00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:52.310
to take care of patients.

00:15:52.310 --> 00:15:55.580
Both COVID patients
and non COVID patients.

00:15:55.580 --> 00:16:00.580
So, if we become sick or
exposed with a fever, stay home.

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:04.124
If you do get COVID-19
because many of us will

00:16:04.124 --> 00:16:05.790
actually become infected.

00:16:05.790 --> 00:16:09.840
And have a relatively
mild case of COVID-19.

00:16:09.840 --> 00:16:12.911
Please stay home until you've recovered.

00:16:12.911 --> 00:16:15.710
And just take care of your mental health

00:16:15.710 --> 00:16:20.080
and of each other, thank you.

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:20.920
- Thanks Ann.

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:23.610
Our next speaker is Rajita Sinha.

00:16:23.610 --> 00:16:24.950
And she's gonna talk about how we can cope

00:16:24.950 --> 00:16:26.280
with the changes we're being asked to make

00:16:26.280 --> 00:16:28.614
in our lives which are
pretty extraordinary.

00:16:28.614 --> 00:16:32.590
Even for many events
that we're experienced

00:16:32.590 --> 00:16:34.523
over the course of our lifetimes.

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:36.943
- Thank you Gregg.

00:16:36.943 --> 00:16:39.920
It's a pleasure to be with you all

00:16:39.920 --> 00:16:42.610
even though these are
difficult circumstances.

00:16:42.610 --> 00:16:45.410
And to get a chance to talk about this.

00:16:45.410 --> 00:16:48.910
Indeed we have been asked
to make some rapid changes

00:16:48.910 --> 00:16:52.240
in our lives in a short period of time.

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:55.760
And you've already begun
to hear about some of them.

00:16:55.760 --> 00:16:58.591
The first one you just heard
about was social distancing.

00:16:58.591 --> 00:17:03.591
Really a number of
recommendations like that

00:17:03.774 --> 00:17:08.774
to reduce the risk and to
prevent getting exposed.

00:17:09.620 --> 00:17:13.120
The psychological side
of social distancing

00:17:13.120 --> 00:17:16.630
and pulling back from your net community

00:17:16.630 --> 00:17:20.340
and your social network can be difficult.

00:17:20.340 --> 00:17:24.570
Particularly in the context of having to

00:17:24.570 --> 00:17:28.440
and not actually connect socially,

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:30.728
physically with loved ones.

00:17:30.728 --> 00:17:32.870
And so, one of the things that we want to

00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:36.540
remind people to do is
to still stay connected

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:41.540
using FaceTime, using other
technology and social media.

00:17:44.330 --> 00:17:47.050
And really stay connected with the family,

00:17:47.050 --> 00:17:47.883
with your friends.

00:17:47.883 --> 00:17:50.540
To get social support
because social support

00:17:50.540 --> 00:17:54.700
is a really important aspect of offering

00:17:54.700 --> 00:17:57.970
under stressful conditions.

00:17:57.970 --> 00:18:00.380
The second thing that
you heard about already

00:18:00.380 --> 00:18:03.850
is that workplaces are
changing and asking people

00:18:03.850 --> 00:18:05.830
to stay home or not come in.

00:18:05.830 --> 00:18:08.800
And there are lots of challenging

00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:11.230
and difficult fallout from that.

00:18:11.230 --> 00:18:14.120
Either loosing pay which
you've heard about.

00:18:14.120 --> 00:18:19.120
But also suddenly finding
yourself being in isolation

00:18:19.140 --> 00:18:21.360
if the workplace is a place where you are,

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:23.230
in fact, connecting with others.

00:18:23.230 --> 00:18:26.160
So, feeling a sense of disconnectedness.

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:30.700
On the one hand it can be
rejuvenating in terms of time

00:18:30.700 --> 00:18:32.210
and you could get more work done.

00:18:32.210 --> 00:18:35.740
On the other hand it can be disconnecting.

00:18:35.740 --> 00:18:39.310
And so, it's really important to really

00:18:39.310 --> 00:18:43.140
develop a schedule to list the
kinds of things you can do.

00:18:43.140 --> 00:18:46.050
And again, connect with others.

00:18:46.050 --> 00:18:47.770
The final thing that I want to talk about

00:18:47.770 --> 00:18:49.320
which I think is a big one.

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:52.600
Which is when you don't
know who is going to get it,

00:18:52.600 --> 00:18:53.840
whether you're going to get it.

00:18:53.840 --> 00:18:55.750
Whether your family's gonna get infected.

00:18:55.750 --> 00:18:57.530
When it will be here.

00:18:57.530 --> 00:19:00.910
That really increases our fear and anxiety

00:19:00.910 --> 00:19:03.000
and a sense of panic.

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:05.970
And so those emotions are
difficult to cope with.

00:19:05.970 --> 00:19:07.674
They increase our stress levels.

00:19:07.674 --> 00:19:10.210
And it's important to acknowledge that.

00:19:10.210 --> 00:19:13.553
To start by really accepting
that this is a difficult time

00:19:13.553 --> 00:19:17.120
and there's a lot of
things that we don't know.

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:21.147
And so, it's important to pay
attention to recommendations.

00:19:21.147 --> 00:19:25.640
But also not overwhelm ourselves
with news and information.

00:19:25.640 --> 00:19:28.680
Sometimes you might get
involved in a lot of conflicting

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:30.780
pieces of information.

00:19:30.780 --> 00:19:34.400
And that can create confusion
about what to listen to.

00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:37.920
And so, as you already
heard, you want to focus

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:42.920
on the things, reliable sources of news.

00:19:44.530 --> 00:19:46.770
So, in the spirit of
that let me just give you

00:19:46.770 --> 00:19:50.297
a couple of points of resources.

00:19:50.297 --> 00:19:53.720
So, the Centers for Disease
Control actually has

00:19:53.720 --> 00:19:58.720
how to manage life and the
changes during this period.

00:20:00.040 --> 00:20:03.350
And has a section on
managing stress and anxiety.

00:20:03.350 --> 00:20:05.120
So, I urge you to go there.

00:20:05.120 --> 00:20:07.638
The American Psychological Association has

00:20:07.638 --> 00:20:10.513
lots of resources posted on their website.

00:20:10.513 --> 00:20:13.660
The Yale Stress Center here right at Yale

00:20:13.660 --> 00:20:17.510
will be offering a free class
starting next Wednesday.

00:20:17.510 --> 00:20:21.270
For 30 minutes every week
for the next number of weeks

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:24.313
and that's on the
YaleStressCenter.org website.

00:20:25.170 --> 00:20:26.960
And so, there are a
number of resources that

00:20:26.960 --> 00:20:30.970
can provide you with
information on how to cope.

00:20:30.970 --> 00:20:35.370
As well as tools, skills and
strategies on the best ways

00:20:35.370 --> 00:20:37.790
that you can cope that would match your

00:20:37.790 --> 00:20:41.137
particular skills and
abilities, thank you.

00:20:42.720 --> 00:20:43.553
- Thank you.

00:20:43.553 --> 00:20:47.290
Next is Amy Kapczynski who's
gonna talk about the sort of,

00:20:47.290 --> 00:20:48.123
the human rights aspect.

00:20:48.123 --> 00:20:50.570
And the social justice
aspect of this pandemic

00:20:50.570 --> 00:20:52.053
and what's facing us, Amy.

00:20:53.139 --> 00:20:56.823
- Thanks, it's nice to
be here with all of you.

00:20:57.820 --> 00:21:00.190
So Gregg and I have been
working together actually

00:21:00.190 --> 00:21:02.990
with many others on broader rights

00:21:02.990 --> 00:21:04.957
and justice implications of the pandemic.

00:21:04.957 --> 00:21:06.566
Wanted to say a couple of things.

00:21:06.566 --> 00:21:10.490
First, there's a number
of places where we've

00:21:10.490 --> 00:21:13.830
tried to specify how to protect rights

00:21:13.830 --> 00:21:15.150
in the context of COVID.

00:21:15.150 --> 00:21:16.930
And really what that means
is that our government has to

00:21:16.930 --> 00:21:20.270
step up and provide
protections for people.

00:21:20.270 --> 00:21:23.973
And really create a
surge of social support.

00:21:25.010 --> 00:21:26.830
So, some of this is
detailed in an expert letter

00:21:26.830 --> 00:21:30.620
that Gregg and many others
and I contributed to.

00:21:30.620 --> 00:21:32.900
I'm gonna provide links to
that and a few other things

00:21:32.900 --> 00:21:36.010
along side this video.

00:21:36.010 --> 00:21:38.280
So that people can access in
more details on some of this.

00:21:38.280 --> 00:21:39.740
But just to say a couple of things about

00:21:39.740 --> 00:21:42.140
what's important to think about.

00:21:42.140 --> 00:21:47.120
So, we really have been
concerned for a long time now

00:21:47.120 --> 00:21:49.557
that not enough is being
done to protect our rights

00:21:49.557 --> 00:21:50.630
and to protect people.

00:21:50.630 --> 00:21:53.410
That we need a major
infusion of public support.

00:21:53.410 --> 00:21:54.810
Now, that's under discussion now.

00:21:54.810 --> 00:21:58.140
We really are starting to see
some action in that regard,

00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:00.900
both nationally and locally.

00:22:00.900 --> 00:22:04.250
We need, obviously, to
think about how to increase

00:22:04.250 --> 00:22:07.020
our capacities with respect to hospitals.

00:22:07.020 --> 00:22:09.180
How to protect healthcare workers.

00:22:09.180 --> 00:22:12.120
And how to protect everybody
who's going to seek healthcare.

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:14.347
For example, by ensuring that nobody fears

00:22:14.347 --> 00:22:16.680
immigration enforcement if
they're seeking healthcare.

00:22:16.680 --> 00:22:19.960
Or fears that contact with
public health officials

00:22:19.960 --> 00:22:21.910
put them at risk.

00:22:21.910 --> 00:22:26.910
So, we also need to be able to
have science based responses.

00:22:28.250 --> 00:22:30.143
So, there's a lot of, of
course, action going on.

00:22:30.143 --> 00:22:32.350
And things are changing very, very quickly

00:22:32.350 --> 00:22:34.660
with respect to what the states are doing.

00:22:34.660 --> 00:22:37.060
What localities are
doing and, more slowly,

00:22:37.060 --> 00:22:40.310
the federal government is
doing to try to respond.

00:22:40.310 --> 00:22:42.530
So, broadly speaking the
state has pretty broad

00:22:42.530 --> 00:22:45.720
public health powers to
require things like quarantine

00:22:45.720 --> 00:22:47.210
or other social distancing measures.

00:22:47.210 --> 00:22:49.730
So, people are probably
wondering what can the state do?

00:22:49.730 --> 00:22:50.760
How far can it go?

00:22:50.760 --> 00:22:52.240
What are our rights in that regard?

00:22:52.240 --> 00:22:54.740
So, let me say a little bit about that.

00:22:54.740 --> 00:22:56.570
So, every state has it's own laws

00:22:56.570 --> 00:22:58.810
about powers like quarantine for example.

00:22:58.810 --> 00:23:00.410
But many of them have broad statutes

00:23:00.410 --> 00:23:01.690
that allow them to take measures that are

00:23:01.690 --> 00:23:03.290
necessary for health and safety.

00:23:03.290 --> 00:23:06.350
Many states are asking for cooperation

00:23:06.350 --> 00:23:08.020
and that's a good thing.

00:23:08.020 --> 00:23:09.777
About where they can do things voluntarily

00:23:09.777 --> 00:23:12.620
and not issue orders,
that's a good practice.

00:23:12.620 --> 00:23:14.540
But there are also places now.

00:23:14.540 --> 00:23:16.130
We see what's happening in San Francisco

00:23:16.130 --> 00:23:18.610
with respect in the
shelter-in-place order.

00:23:18.610 --> 00:23:20.940
And many other states
with respect, for example,

00:23:20.940 --> 00:23:23.750
bars, restaurants and schools
are ordering closures.

00:23:23.750 --> 00:23:26.993
And the state does have
broad powers to do that.

00:23:26.993 --> 00:23:29.520
So, if it is the case though
that social distancing

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:31.420
measures restricts rights.

00:23:31.420 --> 00:23:33.560
Then they have to be
especially well justified.

00:23:33.560 --> 00:23:36.410
So, both the state and
federal constitutions

00:23:36.410 --> 00:23:39.060
protect our rights in various ways.

00:23:39.060 --> 00:23:41.780
One thing to know generally
about US law though

00:23:41.780 --> 00:23:45.320
is that the kinds of rights
that we protect are limited.

00:23:45.320 --> 00:23:48.050
So, we protect liberty
very strongly for example.

00:23:48.050 --> 00:23:49.940
But we don't have a right
to food or healthcare

00:23:49.940 --> 00:23:52.230
in our state or federal constitution.

00:23:52.230 --> 00:23:54.550
So, if the state does do
things that restrict liberty.

00:23:54.550 --> 00:23:57.160
For example, ordering
somebody into quarantine.

00:23:57.160 --> 00:23:59.674
There are protections that do exist.

00:23:59.674 --> 00:24:03.200
The law is not, there
have not been that many

00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:05.090
cases about these kinds of things.

00:24:05.090 --> 00:24:07.540
So, it's sort of challenging to set out

00:24:07.540 --> 00:24:09.910
what the legal requirements are.

00:24:09.910 --> 00:24:12.910
But just as a general,
very broad standard.

00:24:12.910 --> 00:24:16.340
If the state's restricting
liberty one of the things.

00:24:16.340 --> 00:24:19.130
Our view of their legal
requirements is that the quarantine.

00:24:19.130 --> 00:24:21.314
If it's a quarantine for
example has to be necessary.

00:24:21.314 --> 00:24:23.760
Justified by science and the least

00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:26.533
intrusive means of achieving an end.

00:24:28.230 --> 00:24:30.400
Even if a measure is
justified the state does also

00:24:30.400 --> 00:24:32.090
need to follow certain
rules when enacting it.

00:24:32.090 --> 00:24:34.450
For example, you have to
provide clear justifications.

00:24:34.450 --> 00:24:36.940
People need notice of
what's expected of them.

00:24:36.940 --> 00:24:38.360
They need information about how to

00:24:38.360 --> 00:24:39.660
challenge any restrictions.

00:24:39.660 --> 00:24:43.433
So you can be allowed some
kind of independent review.

00:24:43.433 --> 00:24:46.393
If you're being, for example,
ordered to stay home.

00:24:47.844 --> 00:24:50.610
Then the state has to give
you adequate care and food.

00:24:50.610 --> 00:24:52.210
So, it's important to
know that those kinds

00:24:52.210 --> 00:24:57.210
of legal supports have to be taken care of

00:24:58.320 --> 00:25:01.863
if the state does do things
that restrict your liberty.

00:25:02.710 --> 00:25:05.240
So, broadly speaking,
another emphasis that we

00:25:05.240 --> 00:25:08.650
have been all trying to pay attention to

00:25:08.650 --> 00:25:10.428
and call for is more social support

00:25:10.428 --> 00:25:13.430
to allow people to follow
public health advice.

00:25:13.430 --> 00:25:18.430
So, broadly speaking there
are all kinds of things

00:25:18.470 --> 00:25:20.160
that we need to do to
be able to get people

00:25:20.160 --> 00:25:22.710
in position to do the
social distancing that,

00:25:22.710 --> 00:25:24.480
of course, we all need to do right now.

00:25:24.480 --> 00:25:26.430
And some of that has to do with providing

00:25:26.430 --> 00:25:29.840
resources directly to effected people.

00:25:29.840 --> 00:25:32.837
And to providing resources to those

00:25:32.837 --> 00:25:36.070
who are hard hit in the community.

00:25:36.070 --> 00:25:37.980
So, a couple things about
that and I'll just speak

00:25:37.980 --> 00:25:40.044
more specifically here about Connecticut.

00:25:40.044 --> 00:25:41.930
Because I know there'll
be a lot of local people

00:25:41.930 --> 00:25:44.510
who are interested in how to think about

00:25:44.510 --> 00:25:46.360
the benefits that are available to them.

00:25:46.360 --> 00:25:48.790
So, I'm mostly gonna summarize
here from some excellent

00:25:48.790 --> 00:25:51.480
information that was provided
to me by Alex Rosenthal

00:25:51.480 --> 00:25:53.660
who works for the Center
for Children's Advocacy.

00:25:53.660 --> 00:25:55.920
And global partnership and Shelley Geballe

00:25:55.920 --> 00:25:57.790
who is Assistant Professor of Clinical

00:25:57.790 --> 00:25:59.170
Public Health at Yale.

00:25:59.170 --> 00:26:01.252
So, many other states will
be doing similar things

00:26:01.252 --> 00:26:03.280
but this is specific to Connecticut now.

00:26:03.280 --> 00:26:05.260
So, unemployment, for example,

00:26:05.260 --> 00:26:07.040
may be available for some people.

00:26:07.040 --> 00:26:09.600
And Connecticut has relaxed
the rules for who is eligible.

00:26:09.600 --> 00:26:11.780
So, right now this state
is encouraging everyone

00:26:11.780 --> 00:26:14.170
who is currently out of
work due to the pandemic

00:26:14.170 --> 00:26:16.023
to file for unemployment ASAP.

00:26:17.270 --> 00:26:18.840
Any worker who receives health insurance

00:26:18.840 --> 00:26:21.380
through their employer
and becomes unemployed.

00:26:21.380 --> 00:26:23.040
And loses minimal essential coverage

00:26:23.040 --> 00:26:24.970
or whose COBRA expires.

00:26:24.970 --> 00:26:26.740
Whether because of COVID-19 or otherwise.

00:26:26.740 --> 00:26:28.950
It's gonna qualify all year
long for open enrollment

00:26:28.950 --> 00:26:30.560
to access Health CT.

00:26:30.560 --> 00:26:33.340
Which is the states health
insurance marketplace.

00:26:33.340 --> 00:26:35.360
There's actually a new
special enrollment period

00:26:35.360 --> 00:26:36.370
that was just announced.

00:26:36.370 --> 00:26:38.880
So that people who are uninsured can now

00:26:38.880 --> 00:26:41.470
from March 19 to April 2nd.

00:26:41.470 --> 00:26:44.937
If you are uninsured you
can enroll now, again,

00:26:44.937 --> 00:26:48.470
through Access CT, Health
CT for health insurance.

00:26:48.470 --> 00:26:50.420
So, there's also financial help available

00:26:50.420 --> 00:26:54.640
for those who are in need.

00:26:54.640 --> 00:26:55.850
With respect to housing.

00:26:55.850 --> 00:26:57.940
Of course, many people
face housing issues.

00:26:57.940 --> 00:27:00.720
And housing is gonna be
critical to being able

00:27:01.912 --> 00:27:05.340
to engage in social distancing
and protect people's health.

00:27:05.340 --> 00:27:07.510
Here there's advocacy going on.

00:27:07.510 --> 00:27:09.300
So far what we know is that there's been

00:27:09.300 --> 00:27:12.398
a suspension of hearings
and defaults in executions

00:27:12.398 --> 00:27:16.440
in Connecticut's Housing
Court until April 1st, 2020.

00:27:16.440 --> 00:27:18.240
So, that means that those facing evictions

00:27:18.240 --> 00:27:19.600
may have some relief.

00:27:19.600 --> 00:27:22.690
It's also been steps taken
to order utility providers

00:27:22.690 --> 00:27:24.310
to suspend shut-offs for the duration

00:27:24.310 --> 00:27:25.980
of the public health emergency.

00:27:25.980 --> 00:27:28.810
Regardless of people's ability to pay.

00:27:28.810 --> 00:27:30.430
Schools are providing meals for children

00:27:30.430 --> 00:27:31.680
who attend their schools.

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:36.610
And for further details on all of these

00:27:36.610 --> 00:27:38.250
issues as well as how people can apply

00:27:38.250 --> 00:27:40.880
for things like cash
assistance and food stamps.

00:27:40.880 --> 00:27:43.260
Follow the links that I'll provide

00:27:43.260 --> 00:27:45.200
associated with this video.

00:27:45.200 --> 00:27:47.370
The last thing I just
wanted to say here is that

00:27:47.370 --> 00:27:49.750
all of this is going to
be evolving very quickly.

00:27:49.750 --> 00:27:52.700
Many of these things may be
out of date in just a few days.

00:27:53.960 --> 00:27:55.660
The last thing I'd urge
you to do is to contact

00:27:55.660 --> 00:27:57.870
your local state and
federal representatives

00:27:57.870 --> 00:27:59.563
to tell them what you need.

00:28:00.460 --> 00:28:03.610
To make yourself heard about
the hardships to your families,

00:28:03.610 --> 00:28:05.640
to your business and to your loved ones.

00:28:05.640 --> 00:28:09.000
And many of our state
legislators are also providing

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:12.870
useful updates on these daily
changes that are now happening

00:28:12.870 --> 00:28:15.303
to try to provide support
for these in this context.

00:28:16.300 --> 00:28:17.680
- Thanks Amy.

00:28:17.680 --> 00:28:19.840
Next we're gonna have Paul
Genecin from Yale Health

00:28:19.840 --> 00:28:21.810
to talk about what the
health plan is doing

00:28:21.810 --> 00:28:25.413
for staff, faculty and
students at Yale, Paul.

00:28:29.847 --> 00:28:31.313
- Thank you Gregg.

00:28:31.313 --> 00:28:32.550
Hello to everyone.

00:28:32.550 --> 00:28:36.410
I'm Paul Genecin, I'm
Director of Yale Health.

00:28:36.410 --> 00:28:40.220
Several weeks ago Yale
President Peter Salovey

00:28:40.220 --> 00:28:44.633
appointed the COVID-19 or
Coronavirus Advisory Committee.

00:28:45.730 --> 00:28:48.360
This 12 member group has quickly become a

00:28:48.360 --> 00:28:52.653
multi disciplinary task
force meeting daily by Zoom.

00:28:53.840 --> 00:28:56.450
We have Yale public health experts.

00:28:56.450 --> 00:28:59.660
Representatives from
Yale New Haven Hospital

00:28:59.660 --> 00:29:01.200
as well as Yale Health.

00:29:01.200 --> 00:29:03.470
And leadership from our
schools of public health,

00:29:03.470 --> 00:29:05.480
nursing and medicine.

00:29:05.480 --> 00:29:07.910
In fact, two of our
members are Dean Ann Kurth

00:29:07.910 --> 00:29:09.363
and Sten Vermund.

00:29:11.620 --> 00:29:15.020
We found many opportunities
to work together

00:29:15.020 --> 00:29:18.150
to track the Coronavirus epidemic

00:29:18.150 --> 00:29:21.390
and prove our responses in university

00:29:21.390 --> 00:29:23.760
as healthcare providers and members of

00:29:23.760 --> 00:29:25.507
the New Haven Community.

00:29:27.420 --> 00:29:29.201
We've recommended public health actions

00:29:29.201 --> 00:29:32.090
to our university leadership to reduce

00:29:32.090 --> 00:29:33.610
the risk of contagion.

00:29:33.610 --> 00:29:37.420
And examples include defining criteria

00:29:37.420 --> 00:29:40.490
for isolating and monitoring individuals.

00:29:40.490 --> 00:29:43.300
And whom the diagnosis of COVID infection

00:29:43.300 --> 00:29:45.600
is suspected or confirmed.

00:29:45.600 --> 00:29:48.730
And developing ways to
care for them at home

00:29:48.730 --> 00:29:50.970
while protecting the community

00:29:50.970 --> 00:29:53.461
while these patients are contagious.

00:29:53.461 --> 00:29:57.880
We've developed ways to
register, isolate, monitor

00:29:57.880 --> 00:30:00.910
returning travelers from high risk areas.

00:30:00.910 --> 00:30:05.753
Examples are China, South Korea,
Iran and now all of Europe.

00:30:06.690 --> 00:30:10.150
We've made recommendations
to reduce gatherings

00:30:10.150 --> 00:30:12.560
to minimize the spread of infection.

00:30:12.560 --> 00:30:16.080
And transition to Zoom
and other technology

00:30:16.080 --> 00:30:18.803
for teaching and meetings and gatherings.

00:30:19.870 --> 00:30:23.160
We've also recommended reducing activity

00:30:23.160 --> 00:30:26.520
across the university to
protect our workforce,

00:30:26.520 --> 00:30:28.070
our students and our community.

00:30:29.927 --> 00:30:34.050
We're working to ensure clear
and reliable communications

00:30:34.050 --> 00:30:35.040
with our community.

00:30:35.040 --> 00:30:39.120
For example, with email
messages online content

00:30:39.120 --> 00:30:41.547
that we update frequently
providing answers

00:30:41.547 --> 00:30:44.190
to frequently asked questions.

00:30:44.190 --> 00:30:47.373
Also advice for people
on how to stay healthy.

00:30:48.212 --> 00:30:51.860
We have instructions for people
with symptoms and concerns.

00:30:51.860 --> 00:30:54.180
Guidance and resource for travelers

00:30:54.180 --> 00:30:57.513
and links to reliable online resources.

00:30:58.930 --> 00:31:02.230
Our group is fostering
communication and collaboration

00:31:02.230 --> 00:31:05.680
between Yale University,
Yale New Haven Hospital,

00:31:05.680 --> 00:31:08.370
our state and local health departments

00:31:08.370 --> 00:31:10.073
and government leaders.

00:31:11.070 --> 00:31:15.580
And we've worked to adapt and
expand patient care capacity

00:31:15.580 --> 00:31:19.137
to respond effectively
to the COVID pandemic.

00:31:19.137 --> 00:31:22.410
One example is opening telephone access

00:31:22.410 --> 00:31:25.360
through a hotline and
call centers to deal with

00:31:25.360 --> 00:31:28.760
many COVID related concerns.

00:31:28.760 --> 00:31:31.180
We've initiated telephone screening

00:31:31.180 --> 00:31:33.694
instructing patients to call first

00:31:33.694 --> 00:31:37.710
before entering healthcare facilities.

00:31:37.710 --> 00:31:41.533
And this allows us to avoid transmitting

00:31:43.560 --> 00:31:44.910
respiratory infection.

00:31:44.910 --> 00:31:47.160
But still allowing us to see patients

00:31:47.160 --> 00:31:49.860
who may have COVID safely.

00:31:49.860 --> 00:31:52.770
We avoid accidental spread of this disease

00:31:52.770 --> 00:31:54.881
to other patients and to staff.

00:31:54.881 --> 00:31:58.300
We've also worked on ways to scale back

00:31:58.300 --> 00:32:00.530
nonessential care encounters.

00:32:00.530 --> 00:32:03.805
And to transition to
telephone, telemedicine

00:32:03.805 --> 00:32:08.420
and in some cases postponing
care that can safely wait.

00:32:08.420 --> 00:32:11.920
And this is maximizing
our access to patients

00:32:11.920 --> 00:32:13.980
with concerns about COVID.

00:32:13.980 --> 00:32:16.056
And is also making sure
that we have time to

00:32:16.056 --> 00:32:19.350
take care of patients with other non-COVID

00:32:19.350 --> 00:32:21.663
related immediate health concerns.

00:32:22.860 --> 00:32:25.540
Our group has focused on
increasing the workforce

00:32:25.540 --> 00:32:28.410
of physicians, nurses and other essential

00:32:28.410 --> 00:32:29.870
healthcare personnel.

00:32:29.870 --> 00:32:32.840
To take care of people,
whether they're at home

00:32:32.840 --> 00:32:35.380
or in outpatient settings such as offices

00:32:35.380 --> 00:32:37.373
or also in the hospital of course.

00:32:38.660 --> 00:32:41.970
Now, you may know that
our country has been slow

00:32:41.970 --> 00:32:44.037
to roll out testing for Coronavirus.

00:32:44.037 --> 00:32:47.210
And so our work group has
put a lot of work into

00:32:47.210 --> 00:32:51.240
diagnostic testing capacity
and testing criteria

00:32:51.240 --> 00:32:54.210
for patients are hospitalized
with acute illness

00:32:54.210 --> 00:32:55.403
as well as others.

00:32:56.430 --> 00:32:59.470
We're also concentrating
on ways to protect

00:32:59.470 --> 00:33:01.610
healthcare providers and their patients

00:33:01.610 --> 00:33:06.110
from the spread of COVID
infection in healthcare settings.

00:33:06.110 --> 00:33:09.970
This of course includes face
masks and other environmental

00:33:09.970 --> 00:33:12.080
and personal protective equipment.

00:33:12.080 --> 00:33:16.240
But also ways to deal effectively
with healthcare providers

00:33:16.240 --> 00:33:18.570
who may have been exposed to infection.

00:33:18.570 --> 00:33:20.450
To make sure that they do not spread

00:33:20.450 --> 00:33:22.440
the infection to others.

00:33:22.440 --> 00:33:25.930
And then last but not
least we are collaborating

00:33:25.930 --> 00:33:28.210
with the health department
and with our colleagues

00:33:28.210 --> 00:33:30.040
at the Yale School of Public Health

00:33:30.040 --> 00:33:33.090
on the important task of concentration.

00:33:33.090 --> 00:33:36.330
To identify and council those exposed to

00:33:36.330 --> 00:33:38.318
non-Coronavirus patients.

00:33:38.318 --> 00:33:41.628
We give these people that
we identify information

00:33:41.628 --> 00:33:44.720
to keep themselves and the community safe.

00:33:44.720 --> 00:33:47.240
And we advice them on how and when to seek

00:33:47.240 --> 00:33:49.420
medical care if they need it.

00:33:49.420 --> 00:33:51.090
I look forward to your questions.

00:33:51.090 --> 00:33:54.063
I hope that everyone stays
safe and well, Gregg.

00:33:54.980 --> 00:33:56.580
- Thanks Paul.

00:33:56.580 --> 00:33:58.400
Next we're gonna hear from Dr. Dalal

00:33:58.400 --> 00:33:59.560
at the City of New Haven about

00:33:59.560 --> 00:34:02.363
what the city is doing, Dr. Dalal.

00:34:04.370 --> 00:34:05.270
- Thank you Gregg.

00:34:06.260 --> 00:34:10.050
I want to start by saying
a word of gratitude

00:34:10.050 --> 00:34:12.450
to all the staff at the city who have been

00:34:12.450 --> 00:34:14.850
putting in immense amount
of hours in relation

00:34:14.850 --> 00:34:17.287
to the response to this epidemic.

00:34:17.287 --> 00:34:21.050
It's a privilege to work under our mayor,

00:34:21.050 --> 00:34:22.260
Mayor Justin Elicker.

00:34:22.260 --> 00:34:25.762
Along side with our Director
of Health in New Haven,

00:34:25.762 --> 00:34:27.433
Dr. Maritza Bond.

00:34:28.290 --> 00:34:32.520
This has been a time of
unprecedented activity

00:34:33.470 --> 00:34:37.810
in response to this crisis.

00:34:37.810 --> 00:34:40.270
And I'd like to say from
the city perspective.

00:34:40.270 --> 00:34:43.075
I break it out into kind of three phases.

00:34:43.075 --> 00:34:47.750
The first phase is when we saw the tsunami

00:34:47.750 --> 00:34:49.700
coming from far away.

00:34:49.700 --> 00:34:52.879
And our main concern at
that point was putting out

00:34:52.879 --> 00:34:56.620
clear communication about
infection precautions.

00:34:56.620 --> 00:35:01.240
While we ramped up a number
of messaging campaigns around,

00:35:01.240 --> 00:35:02.530
focused around flu.

00:35:02.530 --> 00:35:05.580
At the time flu was the main concern

00:35:05.580 --> 00:35:07.220
with respect to public health.

00:35:07.220 --> 00:35:10.630
We knew the messaging around
flu would apply to Coronavirus

00:35:10.630 --> 00:35:12.340
when it hit Connecticut.

00:35:12.340 --> 00:35:16.610
However, the general
public was not as in tune

00:35:16.610 --> 00:35:18.620
with the threat of Coronavirus as the

00:35:18.620 --> 00:35:20.820
public health officials
and the public health

00:35:20.820 --> 00:35:22.600
community was at the time.

00:35:22.600 --> 00:35:25.670
So, we thought that
focusing our message on flu

00:35:25.670 --> 00:35:30.260
in the early phase of the
pandemic was the way to go.

00:35:30.260 --> 00:35:32.210
The second phase hit us pretty hard.

00:35:32.210 --> 00:35:36.860
The second phase I think
I mark by the event

00:35:36.860 --> 00:35:40.310
at Yale that needed to
be canceled because of a

00:35:40.310 --> 00:35:44.470
suspicious case of Coronavirus
in a visiting student

00:35:44.470 --> 00:35:47.180
that turned out not to be a positive case.

00:35:47.180 --> 00:35:49.382
But it heightened the
communities awareness

00:35:49.382 --> 00:35:52.280
and the fact that this
virus was going to hit,

00:35:52.280 --> 00:35:54.550
eventually, our community.

00:35:54.550 --> 00:35:58.280
In that ramp up phase we
escalated the frequency

00:35:58.280 --> 00:35:59.296
of our meetings.

00:35:59.296 --> 00:36:03.310
Both internally with
city staff and externally

00:36:03.310 --> 00:36:04.900
with our partners.

00:36:04.900 --> 00:36:08.250
Continue to ramp up the
education and messaging

00:36:08.250 --> 00:36:11.780
around preventive precautions
that one could take everyday

00:36:11.780 --> 00:36:14.163
that were discussed
previously on this video.

00:36:15.960 --> 00:36:20.950
Then we also started in
the background planning

00:36:20.950 --> 00:36:23.930
what we would do for city operations when

00:36:27.370 --> 00:36:29.623
the Coronavirus would
appear in Connecticut.

00:36:30.570 --> 00:36:33.410
Of course, third phase
right now is the one

00:36:33.410 --> 00:36:35.400
that I would describe
ourselves in right now.

00:36:35.400 --> 00:36:38.680
This is a period of and
it's marked by the fact that

00:36:38.680 --> 00:36:42.290
we do have Coronavirus
in Connecticut escalating

00:36:42.290 --> 00:36:45.620
exponentially just over the last few days.

00:36:45.620 --> 00:36:50.000
This is a period of
intense operational work.

00:36:50.000 --> 00:36:52.625
There's a multi prong
strategy being coordinated

00:36:52.625 --> 00:36:53.910
through the city.

00:36:53.910 --> 00:36:55.460
In coordination with the state,

00:36:55.460 --> 00:36:57.560
in coordination with
Yale New Haven Hospital.

00:36:57.560 --> 00:37:01.220
In coordination with Yale
Health which includes

00:37:01.220 --> 00:37:03.607
not just a traditional
public health response.

00:37:03.607 --> 00:37:08.450
But also standing up a community health

00:37:08.450 --> 00:37:10.620
and well-being strategy.

00:37:10.620 --> 00:37:14.818
Standing up a intense public
communication strategy as well.

00:37:14.818 --> 00:37:18.220
We had our site go live
that dedicated to COVID

00:37:18.220 --> 00:37:21.810
for New Haven just in the last 24 hours.

00:37:21.810 --> 00:37:24.240
That focuses on local level information

00:37:24.240 --> 00:37:26.220
that our community needs to know about

00:37:26.220 --> 00:37:27.773
with respect to COVID-19.

00:37:29.670 --> 00:37:33.840
Our operations, there
are many aspects to it.

00:37:33.840 --> 00:37:37.060
But I would say there's
three major buckets to it.

00:37:37.060 --> 00:37:40.670
The first is a community
well-being strategy

00:37:40.670 --> 00:37:45.510
which focuses on outreach and protection

00:37:45.510 --> 00:37:46.690
of our older adults.

00:37:46.690 --> 00:37:49.670
Including intense outreach
and technical support

00:37:49.670 --> 00:37:52.440
to facilities that house older adults,

00:37:52.440 --> 00:37:54.240
such as nursing homes.

00:37:54.240 --> 00:37:57.930
But also multi unit dwellings
where a lot of adults live

00:37:57.930 --> 00:38:00.280
potentially in subsidized
housing or in NORCs,

00:38:00.280 --> 00:38:02.640
naturally occurring
retirement communities.

00:38:02.640 --> 00:38:07.460
We are standing a community
wide food distribution strategy.

00:38:07.460 --> 00:38:09.670
The New Haven Public
Schools had led the way

00:38:09.670 --> 00:38:12.590
with respect to making sure that the kids

00:38:12.590 --> 00:38:14.910
who are no longer in
school are receiving meals

00:38:14.910 --> 00:38:17.270
or have the opportunity
to receiving meals.

00:38:17.270 --> 00:38:20.410
We understand that when we talk about

00:38:20.410 --> 00:38:25.060
older adults and even more
or less the entire population

00:38:25.060 --> 00:38:27.300
advising you to stay home and not gather.

00:38:27.300 --> 00:38:31.180
Not everybody's gonna have
the opportunity to eat.

00:38:31.180 --> 00:38:33.710
They rely on food pantries or other

00:38:33.710 --> 00:38:35.280
food distribution services.

00:38:35.280 --> 00:38:37.480
So, we're working on
standing up a community wide

00:38:37.480 --> 00:38:39.220
food distribution strategy.

00:38:39.220 --> 00:38:43.510
We have a specific
strategy that's targeted

00:38:43.510 --> 00:38:44.909
towards homeless individuals.

00:38:44.909 --> 00:38:48.460
They are in a very unique
and difficult circumstance

00:38:48.460 --> 00:38:51.130
that if they are told
that they have COVID-19

00:38:51.130 --> 00:38:53.150
or they are suspected have COVID- 19

00:38:53.150 --> 00:38:57.870
but do not meet acuity
level for hospitalization.

00:38:57.870 --> 00:39:00.150
They do not have a place to self isolate.

00:39:00.150 --> 00:39:02.350
So, we are looking to
stand up a quarantine

00:39:02.350 --> 00:39:04.380
facility for that purpose.

00:39:04.380 --> 00:39:07.060
And finally, we have an
emerging mental health

00:39:07.060 --> 00:39:11.060
and well-being strategy headed
by a partner organization.

00:39:11.060 --> 00:39:13.450
That will be looking at the
mental health well-being

00:39:13.450 --> 00:39:17.340
aspects of our unprecedented
situation where we are

00:39:17.340 --> 00:39:19.810
asking individuals to stay at home.

00:39:19.810 --> 00:39:24.040
Whether through recommendation
by a medical professional

00:39:24.040 --> 00:39:25.480
or public health professional.

00:39:25.480 --> 00:39:27.140
Whether through public health order

00:39:27.140 --> 00:39:29.720
or whether just a general recommendation

00:39:29.720 --> 00:39:31.293
to not gather into groups.

00:39:33.490 --> 00:39:36.750
The second bucket is our
public health response.

00:39:36.750 --> 00:39:40.493
This is the piece of the bread and butter.

00:39:40.493 --> 00:39:43.690
What public health trained
for with respect to

00:39:43.690 --> 00:39:45.440
identifying positive cases.

00:39:45.440 --> 00:39:48.580
Doing the contact, tracing
those positive cases.

00:39:48.580 --> 00:39:51.710
Advising and counseling
those who may not be exposed.

00:39:51.710 --> 00:39:55.940
Assessing the exposure risks
and counseling accordingly.

00:39:55.940 --> 00:40:00.670
In this bucket includes our on-going daily

00:40:00.670 --> 00:40:02.220
or more than daily.

00:40:02.220 --> 00:40:04.600
I would say on-going
everyday communication

00:40:04.600 --> 00:40:05.920
with our health systems partners.

00:40:05.920 --> 00:40:08.992
That includes Yale Health
and Yale New Haven Hospital.

00:40:08.992 --> 00:40:11.840
That collaboration has
been very much appreciated.

00:40:11.840 --> 00:40:16.410
And we need to have this
on-going collaboration

00:40:16.410 --> 00:40:18.460
in order to respond effectively.

00:40:18.460 --> 00:40:21.790
And finally, if you are
local and you're watching

00:40:21.790 --> 00:40:23.950
and you are in the New Haven community.

00:40:23.950 --> 00:40:27.930
You know that over the
last week we have put forth

00:40:27.930 --> 00:40:32.930
an escalating set of policies
that compliment the state

00:40:34.300 --> 00:40:38.040
escalation with respecting to
promoting social distancing.

00:40:38.040 --> 00:40:41.490
Not just through messaging
but through actual policies.

00:40:41.490 --> 00:40:45.100
Some of that includes the school closures.

00:40:45.100 --> 00:40:47.520
The recommendation to close,
actually the requirement

00:40:47.520 --> 00:40:52.023
to close larger daycare
systems and other orders

00:40:53.040 --> 00:40:54.520
that will be forthcoming.

00:40:54.520 --> 00:40:57.110
And finally we have the third bucket

00:40:57.110 --> 00:40:59.294
is a public communication strategy.

00:40:59.294 --> 00:41:02.460
Where we have daily press briefings to

00:41:02.460 --> 00:41:05.680
provide the public
updates on the most recent

00:41:05.680 --> 00:41:10.680
information and education around COVID-19.

00:41:11.730 --> 00:41:15.000
And it's cascading
impacts in our community.

00:41:15.000 --> 00:41:18.300
And it also includes a
component to special populations

00:41:18.300 --> 00:41:21.280
and segmented populations
regarding what they need to know.

00:41:21.280 --> 00:41:24.740
This includes faith leaders,
non-English speakers

00:41:26.570 --> 00:41:29.580
communicating that stigmatization around

00:41:29.580 --> 00:41:31.410
this disease is not acceptable.

00:41:31.410 --> 00:41:34.050
And particular outreach to our businesses

00:41:34.050 --> 00:41:35.640
and our small business community to

00:41:35.640 --> 00:41:37.713
understand their impacts, thank you.

00:41:38.570 --> 00:41:41.680
- Thank you and finally we
have Sanil who's going to

00:41:41.680 --> 00:41:44.150
talk about what's going
on at our hospital,

00:41:44.150 --> 00:41:47.083
Yale New Haven Hospital
here in New Haven, Sanil.

00:41:50.410 --> 00:41:51.460
You're on mute Sanil.

00:41:53.560 --> 00:41:54.900
- Hi, can you hear me?

00:41:54.900 --> 00:41:56.260
- Yep.

00:41:56.260 --> 00:42:00.440
- Excellent, so I will be
probably repeating a bit

00:42:00.440 --> 00:42:03.466
of what Dr. Genecin and Dr. Dalal

00:42:03.466 --> 00:42:05.430
had said in their statements.

00:42:05.430 --> 00:42:07.130
I'm an infections disease physician

00:42:07.130 --> 00:42:09.791
but an Epidemiologist at
the School of Public Health.

00:42:09.791 --> 00:42:13.390
But also see patients at
Yale New Haven Hospital.

00:42:13.390 --> 00:42:16.490
And first of all, I just
want to really give credit

00:42:16.490 --> 00:42:18.550
to the amazing leadership.

00:42:18.550 --> 00:42:20.650
The nurses, the physicians
and the staff at

00:42:20.650 --> 00:42:21.885
Yale New Haven Hospital.

00:42:21.885 --> 00:42:26.885
This has been an extremely
rapidly evolving situation.

00:42:26.930 --> 00:42:29.390
A lot of pieces to the
puzzle and I've just been

00:42:29.390 --> 00:42:33.530
really amazed by the work and dedication

00:42:33.530 --> 00:42:35.570
and passion that everyone's
putting into this.

00:42:35.570 --> 00:42:38.059
And really the empathy that everyone has

00:42:38.059 --> 00:42:39.730
for everyone around them.

00:42:39.730 --> 00:42:42.247
So, I'm really speaking on behalf of

00:42:42.247 --> 00:42:44.423
many, many other people.

00:42:45.930 --> 00:42:48.470
I also want to really reassure the public

00:42:48.470 --> 00:42:51.070
that folks really at the hospital system

00:42:51.070 --> 00:42:52.980
are doing everything that they can

00:42:52.980 --> 00:42:54.650
to really curve this epidemic

00:42:54.650 --> 00:42:57.890
and take the best care of those effected.

00:42:57.890 --> 00:43:00.360
I know there's a lot of worry out there

00:43:00.360 --> 00:43:01.610
and appropriately so.

00:43:01.610 --> 00:43:05.510
With not having tried and true treatments

00:43:05.510 --> 00:43:06.960
available for this disease.

00:43:06.960 --> 00:43:10.903
But folks are really doing
their best for supportive care.

00:43:12.880 --> 00:43:15.803
And again, it's a very
rapidly evolving situation.

00:43:16.650 --> 00:43:18.030
Just to kind of highlight that.

00:43:18.030 --> 00:43:22.750
We had really our first positive case.

00:43:22.750 --> 00:43:25.650
It began this weekend really
with our first positive case

00:43:25.650 --> 00:43:27.520
at Yale New Haven Hospital.

00:43:27.520 --> 00:43:29.320
And at this point we have, I believe,

00:43:29.320 --> 00:43:31.810
about eight in-patients
now at the hospital.

00:43:31.810 --> 00:43:34.853
So again, things changing very rapidly.

00:43:37.100 --> 00:43:41.450
I want to, again, let
folks know that those

00:43:41.450 --> 00:43:44.110
entering the hospital system.

00:43:44.110 --> 00:43:49.110
They are really making a lot
of changes on a daily basis.

00:43:49.520 --> 00:43:52.060
You know, has really
established or in the process

00:43:52.060 --> 00:43:56.360
of establishing a floor that all patients

00:43:56.360 --> 00:43:57.960
with COVID will be sent to.

00:43:57.960 --> 00:44:01.040
And that really has the effect that we see

00:44:01.040 --> 00:44:02.140
in the community as well.

00:44:02.140 --> 00:44:04.340
The social distancing so that within the

00:44:04.340 --> 00:44:07.600
hospital system itself we can really limit

00:44:07.600 --> 00:44:11.030
exposure of other patients who
are there for other reasons.

00:44:11.030 --> 00:44:14.120
And I think that's something that I think

00:44:14.120 --> 00:44:18.360
can provide some comfort to
those outside the system.

00:44:18.360 --> 00:44:21.450
Knowing that at least
patients with COVID will be

00:44:22.530 --> 00:44:25.313
really not spread throughout
the hospital system.

00:44:26.950 --> 00:44:29.243
Other measures I think that Dr. Genecin

00:44:29.243 --> 00:44:33.590
also mentioned is that
visitation to patients

00:44:33.590 --> 00:44:36.910
has been limited and restricted.

00:44:36.910 --> 00:44:39.210
With certain floors having a little bit

00:44:39.210 --> 00:44:40.580
more of a liberal policy.

00:44:40.580 --> 00:44:43.730
Such as pediatrics where
interaction with patients

00:44:43.730 --> 00:44:46.060
are necessary of course.

00:44:46.060 --> 00:44:49.960
Again, that is really
focused on us being able

00:44:49.960 --> 00:44:51.963
to limit the spread of the disease.

00:44:53.080 --> 00:44:56.190
They're making adjustments to the Smilow,

00:44:56.190 --> 00:44:57.219
the cancer hospital.

00:44:57.219 --> 00:45:01.489
Surgical floors in terms of
outpatient elective surgeries

00:45:01.489 --> 00:45:03.280
and whether they should be pursued.

00:45:03.280 --> 00:45:07.900
And really all different
aspects of medical

00:45:07.900 --> 00:45:11.044
and surgical care being
readdressed on literally

00:45:11.044 --> 00:45:13.393
an hour by hour basis.

00:45:14.860 --> 00:45:16.460
I'm sure many are worried about the

00:45:16.460 --> 00:45:18.810
workforce situation as we are.

00:45:18.810 --> 00:45:21.000
You know, for many of these epidemics

00:45:21.860 --> 00:45:24.570
the healthcare workers are the ones that

00:45:24.570 --> 00:45:27.657
end up getting effected early on

00:45:27.657 --> 00:45:29.420
and over the course of the disease.

00:45:29.420 --> 00:45:34.080
But, a workforce plan has
really been developed to

00:45:34.080 --> 00:45:37.633
mitigate the risk of staff
shortages through this epidemic.

00:45:38.970 --> 00:45:42.060
I think Dr. Genecin also
mentioned the telehealth visits.

00:45:42.060 --> 00:45:44.110
And I think that's gonna
be another powerful way

00:45:44.110 --> 00:45:47.993
to really limit patient
exposure into the hospitals.

00:45:48.893 --> 00:45:51.360
In terms of those I don't
want to spend too much time

00:45:51.360 --> 00:45:54.100
on just the clinical aspects of things.

00:45:54.100 --> 00:45:56.180
'Cause I think this has
been covered in the news

00:45:56.180 --> 00:45:59.500
and other sources and
maybe raised in question.

00:45:59.500 --> 00:46:02.390
But I think just to
reiterate that the primary

00:46:02.390 --> 00:46:05.030
point of contact should be your physician.

00:46:05.030 --> 00:46:06.790
And that is the person that could really

00:46:06.790 --> 00:46:11.250
help direct where you should go for.

00:46:11.250 --> 00:46:12.930
Whether or not you should go for testing

00:46:12.930 --> 00:46:14.700
and how to proceed from that point on.

00:46:14.700 --> 00:46:18.400
But we're really urging folks not to enter

00:46:18.400 --> 00:46:20.956
the medical system
without first contacting

00:46:20.956 --> 00:46:22.980
their primary physicians.

00:46:22.980 --> 00:46:24.713
Again, to limit exposure.

00:46:25.730 --> 00:46:30.300
One of the most, I think,
helpful things was the

00:46:30.300 --> 00:46:32.180
establishment of a call center.

00:46:32.180 --> 00:46:33.510
And that was really established

00:46:33.510 --> 00:46:35.750
very early on in this process.

00:46:35.750 --> 00:46:39.400
It's being staffed by other individuals.

00:46:39.400 --> 00:46:44.283
Yale Infectious Disease
physicians, trained physicians.

00:46:46.490 --> 00:46:50.320
Early on with the limited
availability of testing.

00:46:50.320 --> 00:46:52.356
They had a very important
role in determining

00:46:52.356 --> 00:46:56.140
or helping to determine
who should be tested.

00:46:56.140 --> 00:46:59.420
But now, I'm happy to
say that Yale New Haven,

00:46:59.420 --> 00:47:02.790
the lab, has been
fantastic and they are now

00:47:02.790 --> 00:47:05.810
able to run testing for inpatients within

00:47:05.810 --> 00:47:07.293
the Yale New Haven system.

00:47:08.240 --> 00:47:10.650
For folks getting tested in the outpatient

00:47:10.650 --> 00:47:11.730
ambulatory system.

00:47:11.730 --> 00:47:13.760
Those are being sent to
a different location.

00:47:13.760 --> 00:47:16.966
But, we now have a significantly
increasing capacity

00:47:16.966 --> 00:47:18.801
for testing throughout the system.

00:47:18.801 --> 00:47:21.610
It's not yet where we'd like it to be

00:47:21.610 --> 00:47:24.720
but it's significantly better than it was

00:47:24.720 --> 00:47:28.163
early on in this pandemic.

00:47:29.170 --> 00:47:32.290
I'd also like to let people
know that we are kind of

00:47:32.290 --> 00:47:36.390
sharing information between one another

00:47:36.390 --> 00:47:37.710
on the clinical services.

00:47:37.710 --> 00:47:41.195
I think almost every department
is having, if not daily,

00:47:41.195 --> 00:47:42.840
multiple times per week.

00:47:42.840 --> 00:47:47.690
Having department wide calls
to share what we're learning

00:47:47.690 --> 00:47:50.810
on an hourly basis, daily basis.

00:47:50.810 --> 00:47:53.760
So that we can provide
good care for those around,

00:47:53.760 --> 00:47:55.590
those presenting.

00:47:55.590 --> 00:47:59.240
And I learned today that this is not just

00:47:59.240 --> 00:48:00.870
occurring even within departments.

00:48:00.870 --> 00:48:04.031
There are folks reaching
out to physicians in China

00:48:04.031 --> 00:48:09.031
and Europe to learn the most
we can from other experiences

00:48:09.227 --> 00:48:12.062
and other places that
have had the epidemic

00:48:12.062 --> 00:48:13.730
hit earlier than we have.

00:48:13.730 --> 00:48:16.790
So, I think we're
learning things in a real

00:48:16.790 --> 00:48:19.210
global way through this epidemic.

00:48:19.210 --> 00:48:22.040
And I'd say kind of the last thing I think

00:48:22.040 --> 00:48:24.260
has been on the mind of
individuals certainly

00:48:24.260 --> 00:48:25.470
is the treatment issue.

00:48:25.470 --> 00:48:30.470
And while there is no
proven treatment for COVID.

00:48:30.770 --> 00:48:34.070
The Yale system has put together both.

00:48:34.070 --> 00:48:36.150
There's individuals as well as groups

00:48:36.150 --> 00:48:38.210
that have been put together to try to look

00:48:38.210 --> 00:48:40.960
into investigational therapies.

00:48:40.960 --> 00:48:44.700
And see if Yale can become
part of clinical trials

00:48:44.700 --> 00:48:47.410
that are gonna be unfolding
in the coming weeks.

00:48:47.410 --> 00:48:52.410
So, I hope that answers some
folks questions, thank you.

00:48:52.990 --> 00:48:53.900
- Thanks everybody.

00:48:53.900 --> 00:48:56.550
It was miraculously on time.

00:48:56.550 --> 00:48:58.363
We should do this online more often.

00:48:59.430 --> 00:49:02.940
So, we opened an email box for questions

00:49:02.940 --> 00:49:05.610
around 9:30 am this morning.

00:49:05.610 --> 00:49:08.860
And by 4:30 pm there were
close to 400 questions

00:49:08.860 --> 00:49:10.650
from all around the world.

00:49:10.650 --> 00:49:13.340
I don't think we have time to
go through all 400 questions.

00:49:13.340 --> 00:49:16.710
So, I pulled out a
representative sampling of them

00:49:16.710 --> 00:49:18.800
that we're gonna try to
go through between now

00:49:18.800 --> 00:49:21.680
and the next 45 minutes that we have left.

00:49:21.680 --> 00:49:25.780
I will read the question then
I'm gonna turn to our panel.

00:49:25.780 --> 00:49:28.480
And if you would like
to answer the question,

00:49:28.480 --> 00:49:29.313
raise your hand.

00:49:29.313 --> 00:49:31.838
If there are two of you we'll go in turn.

00:49:31.838 --> 00:49:35.510
The first question is from
Mura Vangrove from the UK.

00:49:35.510 --> 00:49:37.600
It's one of our more distant questions.

00:49:37.600 --> 00:49:39.539
She said and this is representative of

00:49:39.539 --> 00:49:43.610
several questions that came
in locally in New Haven

00:49:43.610 --> 00:49:45.034
all the way across the country.

00:49:45.034 --> 00:49:47.555
I'm a very healthy 73 year old.

00:49:47.555 --> 00:49:50.250
Why am I being told to self isolate?

00:49:50.250 --> 00:49:52.100
Who would like to take this question?

00:49:54.690 --> 00:49:56.440
Ellen, you have to unmute yourself.

00:49:58.520 --> 00:49:59.660
You're muted, go ahead.

00:49:59.660 --> 00:50:03.190
- Okay, so that is a great question.

00:50:03.190 --> 00:50:06.730
I think what it is is this is a new virus

00:50:06.730 --> 00:50:09.070
in the human population and
we're just learning about it.

00:50:09.070 --> 00:50:10.940
We don't know all the details.

00:50:10.940 --> 00:50:13.910
But one thing we do know
is how different age groups

00:50:13.910 --> 00:50:15.391
have done with this virus.

00:50:15.391 --> 00:50:18.440
And one very interesting thing is

00:50:19.620 --> 00:50:22.020
young people, especially children.

00:50:22.020 --> 00:50:23.960
Even though they get
the virus, do not appear

00:50:23.960 --> 00:50:25.970
to get very sick in general.

00:50:25.970 --> 00:50:27.518
And we also know that the highest risk

00:50:27.518 --> 00:50:30.593
age group is elderly people.

00:50:32.370 --> 00:50:34.760
And people with other health problems.

00:50:34.760 --> 00:50:38.327
So, if you're in the age
category, over the age of 70.

00:50:38.327 --> 00:50:40.510
You're at significantly higher risk

00:50:40.510 --> 00:50:42.480
of having a serious disease.

00:50:42.480 --> 00:50:44.610
Now, as we learn more about the virus

00:50:44.610 --> 00:50:46.680
maybe we could break that down into

00:50:47.880 --> 00:50:50.670
a healthy 73 year old versus a 73 year old

00:50:50.670 --> 00:50:52.110
with this health condition.

00:50:52.110 --> 00:50:53.850
But we don't know that yet.

00:50:53.850 --> 00:50:56.410
All we know is that
people over the age of 70

00:50:56.410 --> 00:50:58.370
are in a very high risk category.

00:50:58.370 --> 00:51:02.320
So, it's a very good idea
to try to not get the virus.

00:51:02.320 --> 00:51:05.630
And the most tried and
true way of doing this,

00:51:05.630 --> 00:51:10.630
even though it seems rather
dull, is to just avoid exposure.

00:51:13.770 --> 00:51:15.200
- Thanks Ellen.

00:51:15.200 --> 00:51:16.221
The next question we have--

00:51:16.221 --> 00:51:17.848
- Can I add to that?

00:51:17.848 --> 00:51:19.220
- Go ahead, go ahead Stan.

00:51:19.220 --> 00:51:22.640
- Mortality rates in
early data for Wuhan China

00:51:22.640 --> 00:51:27.640
were less than a fraction
of 1% in young people.

00:51:28.270 --> 00:51:31.290
But 15% in persons over 70.

00:51:31.290 --> 00:51:34.280
Similar data from Korea.

00:51:34.280 --> 00:51:38.790
Were not as alarming but still death rates

00:51:38.790 --> 00:51:42.340
were over 6% in Koreans.

00:51:42.340 --> 00:51:46.830
And we're seeing some
data in Italy that also

00:51:46.830 --> 00:51:50.870
suggests what Professor
Foxman was highlighting.

00:51:50.870 --> 00:51:52.813
So, yes, some of those people have

00:51:52.813 --> 00:51:55.390
underlined medical conditions.

00:51:55.390 --> 00:52:00.280
But as someone who's over
the age of 65 myself.

00:52:00.280 --> 00:52:03.400
I can tell you that the
evidence is that we do lose

00:52:04.280 --> 00:52:08.810
immune vigor just as a
natural part of senescence,

00:52:08.810 --> 00:52:11.080
as a natural part of aging.

00:52:11.080 --> 00:52:13.590
That's why we recommend
things like pneumococcal

00:52:13.590 --> 00:52:16.590
vaccine in people over 65.

00:52:16.590 --> 00:52:21.590
And other vaccines that are,
like the shingles vaccine

00:52:25.490 --> 00:52:27.770
that are going to afflict you more often

00:52:27.770 --> 00:52:28.790
if you're a little older.

00:52:28.790 --> 00:52:30.714
And just age can be associated with the

00:52:30.714 --> 00:52:33.444
less robust immune system.

00:52:33.444 --> 00:52:34.440
- Thanks.

00:52:34.440 --> 00:52:35.564
- I think--

00:52:35.564 --> 00:52:36.590
- Sanil.

00:52:36.590 --> 00:52:38.420
- Just a second here.

00:52:39.547 --> 00:52:41.305
Piggy backing on that, I think we have

00:52:41.305 --> 00:52:43.520
examples even from the United States

00:52:43.520 --> 00:52:46.800
with the unfortunate situation in Seattle

00:52:46.800 --> 00:52:47.880
with the nursing homes.

00:52:47.880 --> 00:52:50.017
So, you know, largely elderly population.

00:52:50.017 --> 00:52:52.907
And that had a mortality
much higher than we had seen

00:52:52.907 --> 00:52:54.993
really in any other situation.

00:52:56.960 --> 00:52:58.780
- Thanks, I'm gonna go
into the next question

00:52:58.780 --> 00:53:01.010
from Chris George who is a New Havener

00:53:01.010 --> 00:53:03.210
and happens to be the
Executive Director of IRIS.

00:53:03.210 --> 00:53:06.160
The Integrated Refugee
and Immigrant Services

00:53:06.160 --> 00:53:07.640
organization here.

00:53:07.640 --> 00:53:10.220
He has a question, I'm just gonna read it.

00:53:10.220 --> 00:53:11.900
Thank you for your heroic efforts to

00:53:11.900 --> 00:53:13.820
deal with the COVID-19 outbreak.

00:53:13.820 --> 00:53:15.990
Having worked in partnership
with Yale New Haven Hospital

00:53:15.990 --> 00:53:17.980
for more than 15 years to serve the needs

00:53:17.980 --> 00:53:20.184
of immigrant communities,
particularly refugees.

00:53:20.184 --> 00:53:22.010
IRIS is grateful for your commitment

00:53:22.010 --> 00:53:23.493
in high quality services.

00:53:24.600 --> 00:53:25.560
Although we don't have anyone from

00:53:25.560 --> 00:53:27.450
Yale New Haven Hospital here.

00:53:27.450 --> 00:53:29.140
It says there's some
confusion about provision

00:53:29.140 --> 00:53:31.700
of free services for
COVID-19 related issues

00:53:31.700 --> 00:53:33.053
to uninsured patients.

00:53:34.200 --> 00:53:36.550
I'm sure I don't need to remind
you how important this is

00:53:36.550 --> 00:53:38.980
to remove barriers to
healthcare at this time.

00:53:38.980 --> 00:53:41.110
What's the best way to get
priority on this issue?

00:53:41.110 --> 00:53:43.670
Does anyone here have a sense about

00:53:43.670 --> 00:53:46.520
the status of the uninsured
and seeking care in the city?

00:53:49.020 --> 00:53:49.983
Does anyone know?

00:53:54.470 --> 00:53:56.970
- Well, I think it's fair to say that

00:53:56.970 --> 00:54:00.741
if Yale New Haven Hospital is contacted

00:54:00.741 --> 00:54:04.500
with a potential Coronavirus case.

00:54:04.500 --> 00:54:07.890
That case will be
triaged like anyone else,

00:54:07.890 --> 00:54:12.490
regardless of insurance status.

00:54:12.490 --> 00:54:15.240
And that would go for a
trauma victim or anybody else.

00:54:15.240 --> 00:54:20.240
So, I acknowledge that
getting a bill from a hospital

00:54:20.320 --> 00:54:24.240
at the end of a day is
immensely burdensome

00:54:24.240 --> 00:54:25.870
for people without health insurance.

00:54:25.870 --> 00:54:29.720
One of the fatal flaws of
our American health system.

00:54:29.720 --> 00:54:32.756
That we have so many
people without insurance.

00:54:32.756 --> 00:54:36.990
But, nobody's going to
be turned away for care

00:54:36.990 --> 00:54:39.850
because of lack if insurance.

00:54:39.850 --> 00:54:42.740
- I do also want to add
that I believe that now

00:54:44.140 --> 00:54:46.173
the state has assured us that DSS

00:54:47.110 --> 00:54:50.050
will cover the cost of testing for people

00:54:50.050 --> 00:54:51.710
on Medicaid and HUSKY and also for those

00:54:51.710 --> 00:54:52.903
who are undocumented.

00:54:53.920 --> 00:54:55.263
And that's just testing.

00:54:56.660 --> 00:54:58.620
But as Sten said, it's
important for people to know

00:54:58.620 --> 00:55:00.090
that they can get care.

00:55:00.090 --> 00:55:05.090
And we are seeing a lot
that's on the table now

00:55:05.980 --> 00:55:06.860
at the federal level.

00:55:06.860 --> 00:55:08.530
If the federal government
provides more support,

00:55:08.530 --> 00:55:11.640
the states will be able
to provide more support.

00:55:11.640 --> 00:55:13.750
But I do hope we can also get
somebody from the hospital

00:55:13.750 --> 00:55:16.050
to speak directly to sort
of what their policies are

00:55:16.050 --> 00:55:17.750
with respect to these communities.

00:55:19.740 --> 00:55:22.220
- [Gregg] I think I saw
Dr. Dalal's hand up.

00:55:22.220 --> 00:55:26.660
- Yeah, I just wanted to notify
for the New Haven community.

00:55:26.660 --> 00:55:28.910
Both our community health centers are

00:55:28.910 --> 00:55:30.610
online with testing as well.

00:55:30.610 --> 00:55:32.370
So, if there's trouble
accessing the testing

00:55:32.370 --> 00:55:33.910
at Yale New Haven Hospital.

00:55:33.910 --> 00:55:38.910
And they can access the
community health centers,

00:55:39.360 --> 00:55:41.073
they are also doing testing.

00:55:43.640 --> 00:55:48.640
- Okay, so the next question
is from Molly Connelly

00:55:49.670 --> 00:55:51.490
who's asking what daily changes should

00:55:51.490 --> 00:55:53.080
pregnant people be making?

00:55:53.080 --> 00:55:57.403
This came up, the question of
the risk to pregnant women.

00:55:59.430 --> 00:56:02.100
It was 5% or more of the
questions that came in

00:56:02.100 --> 00:56:03.530
over the past days.

00:56:03.530 --> 00:56:06.663
So, can anybody offer some guidance here?

00:56:09.920 --> 00:56:11.220
Sten.

00:56:11.220 --> 00:56:14.770
- The evidence from China and Korea is

00:56:14.770 --> 00:56:18.460
they have not seen untoward
events in pregnant women.

00:56:18.460 --> 00:56:21.130
It does not seem to be more
severe in pregnant woman,

00:56:21.130 --> 00:56:22.591
nor is it less severe.

00:56:22.591 --> 00:56:27.370
So, clearly being younger is an advantage

00:56:27.370 --> 00:56:28.640
for pregnant women.

00:56:28.640 --> 00:56:31.960
Compared to being over the
age of 70, for example.

00:56:31.960 --> 00:56:36.320
Now, we do not have good data on someone

00:56:36.320 --> 00:56:40.960
who is infected in the first
trimester of pregnancy,

00:56:40.960 --> 00:56:43.170
those first three months after conception.

00:56:43.170 --> 00:56:46.050
So, that remains an open question.

00:56:46.050 --> 00:56:49.840
However, pregnancy
management has been the same

00:56:49.840 --> 00:56:51.980
for infected individuals.

00:56:51.980 --> 00:56:54.710
I can tell you as a
pediatrician we don't want

00:56:54.710 --> 00:56:56.970
pregnant women getting
infected with anything.

00:56:56.970 --> 00:57:01.970
So, cautious approaches
to pregnancy and exposure

00:57:02.970 --> 00:57:05.210
are in order as they are for all of us.

00:57:05.210 --> 00:57:07.940
But pregnant women should
be especially careful

00:57:07.940 --> 00:57:09.683
with social distancing.

00:57:12.540 --> 00:57:14.890
- [Gregg] Anybody else
want to respond to that?

00:57:19.040 --> 00:57:22.400
- I would say in addition
to that the only other

00:57:22.400 --> 00:57:23.960
information we have.

00:57:23.960 --> 00:57:28.200
There's not great information
on what to do with

00:57:28.200 --> 00:57:31.840
breastfeeding and I know that's
come up with individuals.

00:57:31.840 --> 00:57:34.306
But I'd say, again, probably contacting

00:57:34.306 --> 00:57:39.043
your obstetrician in that
situation or pediatrician.

00:57:39.930 --> 00:57:43.060
At the moment there's been
no evidence or no data

00:57:43.060 --> 00:57:46.500
to suggest that it can be
transmitted by breastfeeding.

00:57:46.500 --> 00:57:50.680
However, there's really
limited data on that.

00:57:50.680 --> 00:57:54.900
So, we are encouraging
people to reach out to their

00:57:54.900 --> 00:57:57.710
obstetricians and pediatricians
if they are breastfeeding.

00:57:57.710 --> 00:58:00.563
And they have come down
with symptoms of COVID.

00:58:01.831 --> 00:58:05.053
And to get further guidance at that point.

00:58:06.730 --> 00:58:08.040
- This is Ann just jumping in.

00:58:08.040 --> 00:58:10.870
To also work with their midwives if that's

00:58:10.870 --> 00:58:12.370
who they're delivering with.

00:58:12.370 --> 00:58:14.860
We do want women to have
support throughout pregnancy

00:58:14.860 --> 00:58:19.260
as well as their birth
plan and postpartum.

00:58:19.260 --> 00:58:22.180
Knowing that with the
current hospital restrictions

00:58:22.180 --> 00:58:23.950
around the number of people
who can be in the room.

00:58:23.950 --> 00:58:26.170
There may be some alterations in that.

00:58:26.170 --> 00:58:29.360
But again, to protect
oneself as much as possible.

00:58:29.360 --> 00:58:34.113
To stay healthy during the
pregnancy outside of COVID.

00:58:36.460 --> 00:58:40.920
- So, move on to the next
question which is about

00:58:40.920 --> 00:58:42.607
the effects of isolation
on very young children

00:58:42.607 --> 00:58:45.069
and their caregivers by Faren Tang.

00:58:45.069 --> 00:58:48.050
She asks what might we
expect to see in terms of

00:58:48.050 --> 00:58:50.390
potential effects on the
socio emotional development

00:58:50.390 --> 00:58:53.260
of very young children,
toddlers, preschoolers?

00:58:53.260 --> 00:58:55.860
Of complete physical
isolation from other children

00:58:55.860 --> 00:58:58.410
for potentially prolonged period of time.

00:58:58.410 --> 00:59:00.423
Does anybody have any thoughts on that?

00:59:02.120 --> 00:59:03.813
Dr. Sinha perhaps.

00:59:06.220 --> 00:59:09.570
- So, I can say that
it's actually not good

00:59:09.570 --> 00:59:12.300
to have children be isolated.

00:59:12.300 --> 00:59:16.290
But very young children
are with their parents

00:59:17.340 --> 00:59:19.363
in terms of being at risk.

00:59:20.260 --> 00:59:24.180
If you're isolating, you
have to take the precaution

00:59:24.180 --> 00:59:25.300
of social distancing.

00:59:25.300 --> 00:59:28.150
But that, again, does not prevent you from

00:59:28.150 --> 00:59:31.080
engaging with the children.

00:59:31.080 --> 00:59:33.993
Talking with them,
communicating with them.

00:59:35.410 --> 00:59:40.210
Really expressing a lot
of emotion in other ways.

00:59:41.880 --> 00:59:46.020
And that's very important in these times.

00:59:46.020 --> 00:59:49.410
So, I would say that to
keep up the communication,

00:59:49.410 --> 00:59:51.740
both nonverbal and verbal communication.

00:59:51.740 --> 00:59:55.430
And warmth and love
and belonging is really

00:59:55.430 --> 00:59:57.503
critical in these times.

00:59:59.440 --> 01:00:01.100
- Thank you, anybody else?

01:00:02.770 --> 01:00:05.800
- We're looking for closed units.

01:00:05.800 --> 01:00:09.210
Network theories suggests that the greater

01:00:09.210 --> 01:00:12.760
the outreach that you have the more risk

01:00:12.760 --> 01:00:14.280
you have of getting infected.

01:00:14.280 --> 01:00:18.247
So, if there were next
door neighbors who were

01:00:21.215 --> 01:00:23.510
staying at home, not going out.

01:00:23.510 --> 01:00:28.510
Those two children played
and those families interacted

01:00:28.810 --> 01:00:31.930
preferably with a six
foot social distance.

01:00:31.930 --> 01:00:34.970
That would be highly
preferably compared to

01:00:34.970 --> 01:00:36.940
say a babysitter who's going from house

01:00:36.940 --> 01:00:39.890
to house with different calls.

01:00:39.890 --> 01:00:43.277
So, the notion of a
highly restricted network

01:00:45.546 --> 01:00:50.310
is a very superior idea
than a larger daycare

01:00:50.310 --> 01:00:52.690
with many children from many families.

01:00:52.690 --> 01:00:57.690
So, when we're talking about children

01:00:57.700 --> 01:00:59.900
and their inevitability of running

01:00:59.900 --> 01:01:01.440
next door to their friend.

01:01:01.440 --> 01:01:04.270
What we'd like to do
is have those networks

01:01:04.270 --> 01:01:07.140
be very small and very contained.

01:01:07.140 --> 01:01:10.700
And that will be a much better situation

01:01:10.700 --> 01:01:13.690
than say an alternative to close schools

01:01:13.690 --> 01:01:16.550
where parents who have to go to work

01:01:17.420 --> 01:01:20.340
put their children in
some days, not other days,

01:01:20.340 --> 01:01:21.420
other children come in.

01:01:21.420 --> 01:01:24.510
That sort of thing is
much much more risky.

01:01:24.510 --> 01:01:29.240
So, one has to perhaps limit
the child's friendship group

01:01:29.240 --> 01:01:33.933
to a very, very limited pool
if that's absolutely necessary.

01:01:35.080 --> 01:01:36.230
- Okay, thank you Sten.

01:01:37.600 --> 01:01:39.530
The next question is fairly specific

01:01:39.530 --> 01:01:41.294
and I know who's probably gonna answer it.

01:01:41.294 --> 01:01:44.580
It's from Kate Nyhan who's our Research

01:01:44.580 --> 01:01:46.300
and Education Librarian for public health

01:01:46.300 --> 01:01:47.560
here at Yale University.

01:01:47.560 --> 01:01:50.628
And she asked great to hear
about what legal strategies

01:01:50.628 --> 01:01:52.950
are available to avoid or mitigate delays

01:01:52.950 --> 01:01:55.280
in vaccine development or manufacturing

01:01:55.280 --> 01:01:57.180
with respect to patents and licensing.

01:01:58.756 --> 01:02:00.256
I think that might be for Amy.

01:02:01.620 --> 01:02:04.770
- I worked a lot on these questions about

01:02:04.770 --> 01:02:05.830
patents and so forth.

01:02:05.830 --> 01:02:09.160
And how they can interfere
with both access to medicines

01:02:09.160 --> 01:02:10.360
at the end of the day and also at the

01:02:10.360 --> 01:02:12.310
development of medicines.

01:02:12.310 --> 01:02:15.410
So, one thing to know is that patents are

01:02:15.410 --> 01:02:16.540
granted by the government.

01:02:16.540 --> 01:02:20.273
And what they do is they prevent
anybody who holds a patent.

01:02:21.280 --> 01:02:22.810
So, anybody who holds a patent is allowed

01:02:22.810 --> 01:02:25.010
to prevent others from
using that invention.

01:02:26.199 --> 01:02:29.130
And so, they can get in the way of both

01:02:29.130 --> 01:02:31.843
the kind of free exchange
of scientific information.

01:02:31.843 --> 01:02:34.810
And of people being able to access broadly

01:02:34.810 --> 01:02:36.283
something like a new vaccine.

01:02:37.860 --> 01:02:40.820
There's gonna be a lot going on.

01:02:40.820 --> 01:02:42.200
There is already a lot going on

01:02:42.200 --> 01:02:43.550
in the vaccine development space

01:02:43.550 --> 01:02:46.593
and in the development of therapeutics.

01:02:47.760 --> 01:02:49.771
We've all been hearing the same account

01:02:49.771 --> 01:02:51.750
and it's what I believe to be the case

01:02:51.750 --> 01:02:53.400
that we're talking at least a year

01:02:53.400 --> 01:02:55.780
for any kind of vaccine.

01:02:55.780 --> 01:02:58.980
Perhaps therapeutics could happen faster.

01:02:58.980 --> 01:03:03.240
But one of the things
that it's useful to know

01:03:03.240 --> 01:03:05.310
is that the government
does have the authority

01:03:05.310 --> 01:03:06.320
if patents are getting in the way

01:03:06.320 --> 01:03:08.050
with respect to the access.

01:03:08.050 --> 01:03:09.600
At the end of the day
when something's developed

01:03:09.600 --> 01:03:12.610
or with respect to
developing new technology,

01:03:12.610 --> 01:03:14.890
a vaccine or a treatment.

01:03:14.890 --> 01:03:17.700
They have the ability to say that people

01:03:17.700 --> 01:03:18.533
have to collaborate.

01:03:18.533 --> 01:03:20.670
And that we can override patents

01:03:20.670 --> 01:03:23.130
and provide royalties effectively.

01:03:23.130 --> 01:03:27.000
And so that's not a right
that the US government

01:03:27.000 --> 01:03:27.870
has used very much.

01:03:27.870 --> 01:03:30.860
But one example which
seems very salient now

01:03:30.860 --> 01:03:33.660
is maybe you may remember back to 911

01:03:33.660 --> 01:03:36.366
when there was concern about Anthrax.

01:03:36.366 --> 01:03:41.366
And because Anthrax, to be treated

01:03:41.910 --> 01:03:45.080
you needed a drug called Cipro

01:03:45.080 --> 01:03:49.110
which is owned by a company called Bayer.

01:03:49.110 --> 01:03:50.820
They were the ones that held the patent.

01:03:50.820 --> 01:03:52.880
They couldn't supply sufficient amount

01:03:52.880 --> 01:03:55.630
to the federal government
and prices were very high.

01:03:55.630 --> 01:03:56.870
This was obviously sort of an opportune

01:03:56.870 --> 01:03:57.840
moment for price gouging.

01:03:57.840 --> 01:04:00.660
And government threatened
to use this power

01:04:00.660 --> 01:04:02.030
which I've written a lot about.

01:04:02.030 --> 01:04:04.160
Called the Government Patent Use Power

01:04:04.160 --> 01:04:05.390
to say we're gonna override your patent.

01:04:05.390 --> 01:04:07.960
And we're just gonna buy generic
drugs from somewhere else.

01:04:07.960 --> 01:04:10.630
And the company immediately
lowered the price in half

01:04:10.630 --> 01:04:13.960
and made much more
available to the government.

01:04:13.960 --> 01:04:15.580
So, the government does have these powers

01:04:15.580 --> 01:04:17.630
and it'll be important
for our policy makers

01:04:17.630 --> 01:04:20.480
to be thinking ahead if
we're going to be funding,

01:04:20.480 --> 01:04:22.800
as we are now, a lot of
the development efforts.

01:04:22.800 --> 01:04:25.140
To be ensuring that those
come along with guarantees.

01:04:25.140 --> 01:04:27.030
So there'll be access
at the end of the day,

01:04:27.030 --> 01:04:29.370
the taxpayers aren't paying twice.

01:04:29.370 --> 01:04:32.920
Both to fund the treatment
and to then afterwards

01:04:32.920 --> 01:04:36.223
paying exorbitant prices for it.

01:04:36.223 --> 01:04:39.484
It's also important to be paying attention

01:04:39.484 --> 01:04:44.484
to once we do get these
kinds of things developed.

01:04:45.635 --> 01:04:48.350
That the access through these tools

01:04:48.350 --> 01:04:51.112
like government patent use.

01:04:51.112 --> 01:04:51.945
- Thank Amy.

01:04:53.210 --> 01:04:54.810
Gonna move on to our next question is from

01:04:54.810 --> 01:04:57.950
Jason Mento who's a student at
the Yale Student of Nursing.

01:04:57.950 --> 01:05:01.230
Jason asks what services
and efforts exist right now

01:05:01.230 --> 01:05:03.580
for the elderly, the poor
and the recently unemployed

01:05:03.580 --> 01:05:05.750
as a result of the stay at home measures?

01:05:05.750 --> 01:05:07.740
Have we accessed the
needs of these populations

01:05:07.740 --> 01:05:08.620
and can we meet that need?

01:05:08.620 --> 01:05:10.870
And what services exist
to meet those needs

01:05:10.870 --> 01:05:12.193
in the city of New Haven?

01:05:15.040 --> 01:05:17.730
Dr. Dalal, do you want to take that?

01:05:17.730 --> 01:05:18.940
- Yep, just unmuting here.

01:05:18.940 --> 01:05:20.853
Yeah, I'm happy to take that question.

01:05:21.840 --> 01:05:26.840
So, it's important to
remember this type of.

01:05:28.750 --> 01:05:31.460
This has come so fast that providing

01:05:31.460 --> 01:05:35.390
the services requires an entire retooling

01:05:35.390 --> 01:05:38.260
of our current social services network.

01:05:38.260 --> 01:05:40.330
This is happening pretty rapidly.

01:05:40.330 --> 01:05:43.530
But it takes some good times in terms of

01:05:43.530 --> 01:05:46.310
ensuring the logistics will work.

01:05:46.310 --> 01:05:48.850
And not only the logistics
work but the logistics

01:05:48.850 --> 01:05:53.850
are now under the auspicious
of a public health framework.

01:05:54.600 --> 01:05:57.050
So, I'll just start with one example.

01:05:57.050 --> 01:06:01.360
Food distribution would
typically as long as

01:06:01.360 --> 01:06:06.360
it passed a standard set of
public health requirements,

01:06:06.420 --> 01:06:08.980
of sanitary precautions.

01:06:08.980 --> 01:06:12.040
We would sign off on
that as an appropriate

01:06:12.040 --> 01:06:15.610
food distribution program
or food delivery program.

01:06:15.610 --> 01:06:18.790
In this scenario we have to consider the

01:06:18.790 --> 01:06:21.170
social distancing
aspects of food delivery.

01:06:21.170 --> 01:06:23.940
Which means that if we
were to distribute food

01:06:23.940 --> 01:06:25.530
from a certain location.

01:06:25.530 --> 01:06:30.530
Are there the logistic
and aspects planned out

01:06:30.550 --> 01:06:32.380
to the fact that many people don't rush

01:06:32.380 --> 01:06:35.400
to that single site in order
to pick up their meals?

01:06:35.400 --> 01:06:37.590
We also have to think
about the precautions

01:06:37.590 --> 01:06:41.920
that the food delivery
personnel or food distribution

01:06:41.920 --> 01:06:45.700
need to take and educating
them about distance.

01:06:45.700 --> 01:06:48.180
So, there is a ramp up period with respect

01:06:48.180 --> 01:06:50.360
to the availability of these services.

01:06:50.360 --> 01:06:53.040
I mentioned earlier in
my remarks that we have a

01:06:53.040 --> 01:06:57.560
strategy to outreach to
every single facility

01:06:58.410 --> 01:07:00.430
that maybe housing elderly.

01:07:00.430 --> 01:07:05.140
Either in a nursing home
or a congregate setting.

01:07:05.140 --> 01:07:08.680
My hope is that we would,
by congregate setting

01:07:08.680 --> 01:07:12.200
I mean multi unit housing places.

01:07:12.200 --> 01:07:15.430
And my hope is that we would be able to

01:07:17.130 --> 01:07:20.110
muster volunteers to make contact with

01:07:20.110 --> 01:07:23.940
every single senior in
New Haven to understand

01:07:23.940 --> 01:07:25.290
what kind of support they need.

01:07:25.290 --> 01:07:26.840
And if we're assessing their supports.

01:07:26.840 --> 01:07:28.840
To be able to deliver on those supports.

01:07:29.762 --> 01:07:31.290
- [Gregg] Thank you.

01:07:31.290 --> 01:07:34.000
- Gregg, I can add to that.

01:07:34.000 --> 01:07:36.720
Just to remind folks that
there's the Connecticut

01:07:36.720 --> 01:07:40.950
Mental Health Center coping with this risk

01:07:40.950 --> 01:07:44.365
and the worry about it can
be very anxiety provoking.

01:07:44.365 --> 01:07:46.610
And the Connecticut Mental Health Center

01:07:46.610 --> 01:07:48.600
is state supported.

01:07:48.600 --> 01:07:50.480
And available for mental health needs

01:07:50.480 --> 01:07:53.750
but also providing information,
helping people cope

01:07:53.750 --> 01:07:57.110
and also to get to their right services.

01:07:57.110 --> 01:07:59.283
So, that would be an additional resource.

01:08:00.350 --> 01:08:04.660
- Thank you, next question is
also from a nursing student.

01:08:04.660 --> 01:08:08.830
Kelsey Jug, she's saying I'm
a first year student at YSN.

01:08:08.830 --> 01:08:10.555
I'm a babysitter and also a volunteer

01:08:10.555 --> 01:08:12.681
for search capacity in the hospital.

01:08:12.681 --> 01:08:14.910
I've had people tell me I
shouldn't be babysitting

01:08:14.910 --> 01:08:16.280
if I'm gonna be stepping
foot on the floors

01:08:16.280 --> 01:08:18.170
of the hospital at this time.

01:08:18.170 --> 01:08:19.470
Should healthcare workers and hospitals

01:08:19.470 --> 01:08:21.020
give up all social interaction,

01:08:21.020 --> 01:08:23.270
even small groups with social distancing?

01:08:23.270 --> 01:08:25.450
Or should we simply continue
with other responsibilities

01:08:25.450 --> 01:08:27.164
with the necessary
precautions of distancing

01:08:27.164 --> 01:08:29.100
and hand washing, et cetera?

01:08:29.100 --> 01:08:31.520
This is in the context of a bunch of other

01:08:32.600 --> 01:08:36.050
questions about is there
a role for volunteering

01:08:36.050 --> 01:08:38.530
for people who want to do
services for the elderly

01:08:38.530 --> 01:08:40.550
or people who can't otherwise go out?

01:08:40.550 --> 01:08:42.040
This is a very specific one in healthcare

01:08:42.040 --> 01:08:44.670
but you could take this
as a more general question

01:08:44.670 --> 01:08:46.810
or a may specific one about people

01:08:46.810 --> 01:08:49.710
in the healthcare industry,
in the healthcare fields.

01:08:49.710 --> 01:08:51.460
Who would like to answer that, Ann?

01:08:52.350 --> 01:08:55.810
- So, our healthcare workers
are a really precious resource.

01:08:55.810 --> 01:08:58.900
And people talk a lot right
now when we're thinking about

01:08:58.900 --> 01:09:01.740
the potential implications
and more patients

01:09:01.740 --> 01:09:04.090
in the hospital with
acute healthcare needs

01:09:04.090 --> 01:09:07.442
about ICU beds and ventilators.

01:09:07.442 --> 01:09:09.740
Let's not forget the staff who actually

01:09:09.740 --> 01:09:12.710
have to be there 24/7 with patients.

01:09:12.710 --> 01:09:15.150
And so, we are really
thinking quite carefully

01:09:15.150 --> 01:09:17.250
about all of our workforce needs.

01:09:17.250 --> 01:09:20.696
Nurses tend to be, as
with all other pandemics,

01:09:20.696 --> 01:09:23.250
on the frontline spending the most time

01:09:23.250 --> 01:09:26.420
is really in the hospital
settings with patients.

01:09:26.420 --> 01:09:29.130
So, many schools of nursing in Connecticut

01:09:29.130 --> 01:09:32.840
and around the country are
really trying to ensure

01:09:32.840 --> 01:09:36.100
that we can continue the
education of our students

01:09:37.010 --> 01:09:38.310
safely and fairly.

01:09:38.310 --> 01:09:41.050
While also thinking ahead
to some workforce needs.

01:09:41.050 --> 01:09:44.920
Including the opportunity for
students who are licensed,

01:09:44.920 --> 01:09:47.870
who are RNs for example,
not pre-licensure.

01:09:47.870 --> 01:09:50.680
To be able to engage in
some what will be likely

01:09:50.680 --> 01:09:52.960
to be some surge activities.

01:09:52.960 --> 01:09:54.360
The thinking there is that there may be

01:09:54.360 --> 01:09:55.610
different levels of care.

01:09:56.500 --> 01:09:58.694
No patient contact as with phone banks

01:09:58.694 --> 01:10:01.573
and phone triage for example.

01:10:02.620 --> 01:10:05.510
Lower level patient care activities

01:10:05.510 --> 01:10:09.650
and then higher level
acute care activities

01:10:09.650 --> 01:10:12.460
that may involve aerosolizing procedure.

01:10:12.460 --> 01:10:14.190
For all of the care in the hospitals

01:10:14.190 --> 01:10:16.690
and health systems, it just must be said.

01:10:16.690 --> 01:10:19.960
One of the other failings as we dissect

01:10:19.960 --> 01:10:22.860
the lessons from this epidemic.

01:10:22.860 --> 01:10:24.791
Is going to be around
persona protective equipment

01:10:24.791 --> 01:10:29.100
and the constraints and
supply of that equipment,

01:10:29.100 --> 01:10:31.030
PVE that we're calling.

01:10:31.030 --> 01:10:34.376
There really is, that's
really extremely problematic.

01:10:34.376 --> 01:10:36.631
So, we do want to protect
our health workforce

01:10:36.631 --> 01:10:39.389
as they are engaged in
care in the hospital

01:10:39.389 --> 01:10:44.170
or in volunteer activities
in the community.

01:10:44.170 --> 01:10:46.300
And so following standard procedures

01:10:46.300 --> 01:10:48.840
around trying to social distance.

01:10:48.840 --> 01:10:53.840
And stay home if ill, get
treated and recuperate at home

01:10:54.290 --> 01:10:55.123
with a mild case of COVID.

01:10:55.123 --> 01:10:57.760
So that you could even go
back into the health workforce

01:10:57.760 --> 01:10:59.300
because we will be needed.

01:10:59.300 --> 01:11:01.473
Health workforce will
be needed for some time.

01:11:05.310 --> 01:11:07.010
- So, I just want to pivot to a question

01:11:07.010 --> 01:11:08.950
which related from Julien Mortan

01:11:08.950 --> 01:11:11.470
who's from the Yale College class of 2020.

01:11:11.470 --> 01:11:13.590
He says, for those who are able bodied

01:11:13.590 --> 01:11:14.870
relatively young and healthy.

01:11:14.870 --> 01:11:16.300
What can we do to help more vulnerable

01:11:16.300 --> 01:11:18.760
individuals in New Haven
without putting ourselves

01:11:18.760 --> 01:11:21.563
or the larger community
at unnecessary risk?

01:11:22.820 --> 01:11:25.410
So, it's another volunteering question.

01:11:25.410 --> 01:11:26.243
Ellen.

01:11:28.238 --> 01:11:29.430
Unmute.

01:11:29.430 --> 01:11:34.430
- Yes, so I mean one thing
that young people that age

01:11:36.120 --> 01:11:40.557
can do is take the precautions
that we're recommending.

01:11:40.557 --> 01:11:43.260
It's hard, especially at
that age group sometimes

01:11:43.260 --> 01:11:46.940
to have the more social distancing
and to take head of that.

01:11:46.940 --> 01:11:49.280
Especially you hear sometimes young people

01:11:49.280 --> 01:11:50.540
talking about the fact that they don't

01:11:50.540 --> 01:11:52.180
feel as vulnerable to it.

01:11:52.180 --> 01:11:55.860
But I think that sort of
realizing that young people

01:11:55.860 --> 01:11:59.444
can still get infected and
can still spread the virus.

01:11:59.444 --> 01:12:00.923
It's something important.

01:12:00.923 --> 01:12:04.920
It's a self sacrifice
for the broader community

01:12:04.920 --> 01:12:08.123
to try to still practice the
social distancing measures.

01:12:11.310 --> 01:12:14.703
- Anyone else want to speak
to the volunteering question?

01:12:18.950 --> 01:12:23.240
- Yeah, I would say that
probably the very first principle

01:12:23.240 --> 01:12:25.280
and any volunteer engagement that you may

01:12:25.280 --> 01:12:27.860
involve yourself in is to ensure that

01:12:27.860 --> 01:12:29.974
there's a social distancing aspects.

01:12:29.974 --> 01:12:32.700
In terms of needs, I
think we're going to have

01:12:32.700 --> 01:12:35.580
many individuals who are
at home that may not have

01:12:35.580 --> 01:12:37.300
the typical social supports.

01:12:37.300 --> 01:12:39.420
We want to support them to stay at home.

01:12:39.420 --> 01:12:42.640
As I said, whether they are
under medical recommendations

01:12:42.640 --> 01:12:44.430
to stay at home because if COVID.

01:12:44.430 --> 01:12:48.340
Or we are recommending globally
for people to stay at home.

01:12:48.340 --> 01:12:53.310
I think creative ways to be
in touch with individuals

01:12:53.310 --> 01:12:54.900
who are at home.

01:12:54.900 --> 01:12:59.623
Whether that's a telephonic
or video capabilities

01:13:00.584 --> 01:13:03.738
would be excellent
opportunities for volunteering

01:13:03.738 --> 01:13:05.783
for a younger healthy population.

01:13:07.130 --> 01:13:07.963
- [Gregg] Thanks.

01:13:07.963 --> 01:13:10.700
- Could I put a pitch in for tutoring.

01:13:10.700 --> 01:13:12.683
Our children are out of school.

01:13:13.574 --> 01:13:18.320
And you're talking about
kindergarten through 12th grade.

01:13:18.320 --> 01:13:22.820
And tutoring can be very
easily done for families

01:13:22.820 --> 01:13:25.490
who have a computer and internet access

01:13:25.490 --> 01:13:28.860
or even a smart phone and internet access.

01:13:28.860 --> 01:13:30.450
And you can even do tutoring,

01:13:30.450 --> 01:13:32.500
believe it or not, by telephone.

01:13:32.500 --> 01:13:36.200
So, there's a huge need and the
Yale School of Public Health

01:13:36.200 --> 01:13:38.243
is working with the United Way.

01:13:39.100 --> 01:13:44.100
And there is a new website
that the United Way

01:13:44.880 --> 01:13:48.390
has opened up which is
going to permit people

01:13:48.390 --> 01:13:52.840
to make those kinds of
contributions back to the community

01:13:52.840 --> 01:13:54.823
and still maintain social distance.

01:13:56.030 --> 01:13:57.200
- Thanks Sten.

01:13:57.200 --> 01:13:59.080
The next question also
comes from somebody at Yale,

01:13:59.080 --> 01:14:00.500
Victoria Ellison.

01:14:00.500 --> 01:14:03.060
She says why aren't international
domestic flight bans

01:14:03.060 --> 01:14:04.420
being instituted and enforced?

01:14:04.420 --> 01:14:07.163
Should airports enforce
this by shutting down?

01:14:11.450 --> 01:14:12.820
Anybody want to take this?

01:14:12.820 --> 01:14:13.653
Sten.

01:14:14.940 --> 01:14:16.350
- This is a complex issue.

01:14:16.350 --> 01:14:19.100
Let's just first point out that flights

01:14:19.100 --> 01:14:21.260
have been cut radically.

01:14:21.260 --> 01:14:26.113
On some routes they've been
cut from 40 a day to one.

01:14:26.984 --> 01:14:31.330
And there's almost no route domestically

01:14:31.330 --> 01:14:34.263
or globally that has not
been substantially effected.

01:14:35.370 --> 01:14:39.050
You're going to see the
airlines making more

01:14:39.050 --> 01:14:41.913
and more cancellations and adjustments.

01:14:42.750 --> 01:14:46.500
We're hearing stories
of people flying planes

01:14:46.500 --> 01:14:50.720
with 150 passengers and three
seats are being occupied,

01:14:50.720 --> 01:14:52.350
147 vacancies.

01:14:52.350 --> 01:14:57.350
So, there is almost a natural with the

01:14:57.400 --> 01:14:59.040
social isolation recommendations,

01:14:59.040 --> 01:15:04.040
people's concern about
exposure in close spaces.

01:15:04.140 --> 01:15:05.400
People are walking with their feet

01:15:05.400 --> 01:15:07.350
and they're not traveling.

01:15:07.350 --> 01:15:10.410
So, how could I defend
somebody who actually

01:15:10.410 --> 01:15:12.330
does have to travel?

01:15:12.330 --> 01:15:14.760
Some of them are trying to get home.

01:15:14.760 --> 01:15:17.630
Some of them have to leave where they are.

01:15:17.630 --> 01:15:22.260
For example, our diplomats internationally

01:15:22.260 --> 01:15:24.210
are coming home in very large numbers.

01:15:24.210 --> 01:15:27.440
The entire Peace Corps
is being brought home.

01:15:27.440 --> 01:15:32.280
So, there is a global shift
in where people are going.

01:15:32.280 --> 01:15:37.280
I think that routine business
travel, holiday travel.

01:15:37.720 --> 01:15:40.840
Things that can be deferred
should be deferred.

01:15:40.840 --> 01:15:44.440
And I think you'll see
that there is considerable

01:15:44.440 --> 01:15:46.800
social distancing even on airplanes

01:15:46.800 --> 01:15:49.793
and even in airports at the present time.

01:15:50.640 --> 01:15:51.770
- Okay, thanks Sten.

01:15:52.930 --> 01:15:57.100
Next question is from Prashant Emani.

01:15:57.100 --> 01:15:59.480
She says my name is Prashant and I'm an

01:15:59.480 --> 01:16:00.920
Associate Research Scientist at Yale.

01:16:00.920 --> 01:16:02.820
My question is the following.

01:16:02.820 --> 01:16:04.470
What are the protective measures in place

01:16:04.470 --> 01:16:07.200
for New Haven significant
population of homeless people?

01:16:07.200 --> 01:16:09.330
What are Yale and New Haven contributing

01:16:09.330 --> 01:16:10.620
towards the provision of services

01:16:10.620 --> 01:16:12.313
for vulnerable populations?

01:16:14.680 --> 01:16:16.580
Dr. Dalal, you want to take that first?

01:16:16.580 --> 01:16:19.520
- I can start and then
others can weigh in.

01:16:19.520 --> 01:16:22.250
So, one of the strategies
that we have in place

01:16:22.250 --> 01:16:26.560
that we're close to implementing
in terms of logistics

01:16:26.560 --> 01:16:30.380
is a decompression of
the homeless shelters.

01:16:30.380 --> 01:16:33.410
Our homeless shelters were
not equipped to do the

01:16:33.410 --> 01:16:35.590
six feet social distancing.

01:16:35.590 --> 01:16:38.323
And they were also working
at or near capacity.

01:16:38.323 --> 01:16:41.230
So, we realized very
quickly that we needed to

01:16:41.230 --> 01:16:43.970
decompress the shelters
in order to maintain

01:16:43.970 --> 01:16:47.154
the social distance recommendation
within the shelters.

01:16:47.154 --> 01:16:52.154
So, we are about to
bring online hotel rooms

01:16:52.160 --> 01:16:56.800
that will actually house
up to 50 individuals

01:16:57.960 --> 01:17:02.960
that are not known to be COVID
positive into hotel rooms.

01:17:05.500 --> 01:17:08.380
And then for the protection of that

01:17:08.380 --> 01:17:09.880
population in that sense.

01:17:09.880 --> 01:17:12.100
We are also working to stand up a separate

01:17:12.100 --> 01:17:15.280
isolation facility for
homeless individuals

01:17:15.280 --> 01:17:17.170
that do happen to test COVID positive.

01:17:17.170 --> 01:17:22.170
But do not need the acuity
standards for hospitalization.

01:17:24.500 --> 01:17:27.830
We have multiple outreach
efforts that I eluded to earlier

01:17:27.830 --> 01:17:32.350
which includes insuring that
homeless service providers

01:17:33.420 --> 01:17:37.210
know the protocols in respect
to screening individuals.

01:17:37.210 --> 01:17:41.703
And are implementing them to
the best of their ability.

01:17:45.240 --> 01:17:47.823
- Anyone else know Yale's
role in any of this?

01:17:51.820 --> 01:17:52.653
Sten.

01:17:55.070 --> 01:17:57.430
- There's been activity
in our Yale faculty

01:17:57.430 --> 01:17:59.280
working for the VA.

01:17:59.280 --> 01:18:01.710
The VA has done, David
Rosenthal and colleagues

01:18:01.710 --> 01:18:06.320
have done a marvelous
job housing veterans.

01:18:06.320 --> 01:18:09.880
But, sometimes there can there
can be housing and security

01:18:09.880 --> 01:18:12.700
and they've been very
aggressive in following up

01:18:12.700 --> 01:18:13.710
each and every veteran.

01:18:13.710 --> 01:18:17.484
Many of whom are struggling
with chronic medical conditions

01:18:17.484 --> 01:18:22.484
including substance use or
mental health conditions

01:18:22.690 --> 01:18:27.690
or chronic diabetes and the like.

01:18:28.380 --> 01:18:30.990
The Divinity School is
actually getting involved.

01:18:30.990 --> 01:18:34.448
Ms. Cunningham, all the
faculty there used to be

01:18:34.448 --> 01:18:37.740
the Executive Director of Columbus House.

01:18:37.740 --> 01:18:41.520
And she's gotten extremely involved with

01:18:41.520 --> 01:18:46.480
the pragmatics of homeless
housing and mitigation.

01:18:48.140 --> 01:18:53.090
That I think Dr. Sinha
may want to comment.

01:18:53.090 --> 01:18:56.109
We do have a challenge in our city

01:18:56.109 --> 01:19:01.109
in that nexus of homelessness,
poverty, substance use,

01:19:02.170 --> 01:19:06.550
mental health which is
an independent challenge.

01:19:06.550 --> 01:19:08.508
Whether we have Coronavirus or not.

01:19:08.508 --> 01:19:12.400
But I do feel like the Yale community

01:19:12.400 --> 01:19:13.390
is stepping up to the plate.

01:19:13.390 --> 01:19:17.150
I just saw guidelines from
our Addiction Medicine Group

01:19:18.010 --> 01:19:20.850
guiding us exactly how
we would like to tackle

01:19:20.850 --> 01:19:24.380
folks who are struggling
with addiction issues.

01:19:24.380 --> 01:19:27.250
But may be in harms way with Coronavirus

01:19:27.250 --> 01:19:31.060
and strategies to mitigate that harm.

01:19:31.060 --> 01:19:31.893
- Thanks Sten.

01:19:33.000 --> 01:19:35.300
This is a question from Michael Lawler

01:19:35.300 --> 01:19:38.270
who's a Yale College grad from 1970.

01:19:38.270 --> 01:19:41.110
He says given that the symptoms
for all but the elderly,

01:19:41.110 --> 01:19:42.053
which I am.

01:19:43.150 --> 01:19:45.210
And or infirm seem to be trending towards

01:19:45.210 --> 01:19:48.000
mild to moderate symptoms
with high recovery rates.

01:19:48.000 --> 01:19:51.320
And past viral epidemics, swine flu, H1N1.

01:19:51.320 --> 01:19:54.480
In fact, even killed
more people than COVID.

01:19:54.480 --> 01:19:56.020
Please explain why these global actions

01:19:56.020 --> 01:19:57.413
are a rational response to the situation

01:19:57.413 --> 01:19:59.190
and not the greatest case of mass

01:19:59.190 --> 01:20:02.150
hysteria in recent history?

01:20:02.150 --> 01:20:04.600
I think there are a
lot of people who think

01:20:04.600 --> 01:20:06.570
this is all overblown.

01:20:06.570 --> 01:20:08.273
Anybody want to weigh in here?

01:20:10.390 --> 01:20:12.620
- I tried to address that
in my opening comments.

01:20:12.620 --> 01:20:16.590
I really feel like we
should not underestimate

01:20:16.590 --> 01:20:21.440
the risk of a new influenza like disease

01:20:22.400 --> 01:20:25.940
with 10 to 20 times the mortality rates

01:20:25.940 --> 01:20:28.347
being introduced to the human race.

01:20:28.347 --> 01:20:32.000
And it taking hold and
being with us forever.

01:20:32.000 --> 01:20:34.752
That is catastrophic.

01:20:34.752 --> 01:20:39.380
This virus is far better
adapted to human to human spread

01:20:39.380 --> 01:20:44.320
than either the prior
Coronavirus', either SARS or MERS.

01:20:44.320 --> 01:20:47.070
And because we now know
that it's just as good

01:20:47.070 --> 01:20:49.384
at spreading human to human as flu.

01:20:49.384 --> 01:20:53.340
It is worth this effort to try to see

01:20:53.340 --> 01:20:57.910
if we can run into the
summer with extremely

01:20:57.910 --> 01:21:01.330
low levels of ongoing transmission.

01:21:01.330 --> 01:21:06.100
And see if the summer
dynamics will essentially

01:21:06.100 --> 01:21:08.680
scrub it out of the human populations

01:21:08.680 --> 01:21:12.390
the way that we saw SARS in 2004.

01:21:12.390 --> 01:21:15.540
So, that's why I think
it's not mass hysteria

01:21:15.540 --> 01:21:17.490
and I don't think it's an overreaction.

01:21:18.970 --> 01:21:19.943
- Anyone else?

01:21:22.200 --> 01:21:23.033
Ellen.

01:21:25.360 --> 01:21:27.210
- Oh there we go, so yeah.

01:21:27.210 --> 01:21:28.240
I would agree.

01:21:28.240 --> 01:21:32.580
I think this is a
appropriate reaction in that

01:21:32.580 --> 01:21:34.710
also if you look at
what happened in China.

01:21:34.710 --> 01:21:36.640
In the epicenter of the epidemic where

01:21:36.640 --> 01:21:38.396
everyone got sick all at once.

01:21:38.396 --> 01:21:40.850
The mortality rate was much higher than

01:21:40.850 --> 01:21:42.560
in other parts of China.

01:21:42.560 --> 01:21:44.648
And you might wonder why that is.

01:21:44.648 --> 01:21:48.450
It has to do with the fact
that a significant number

01:21:48.450 --> 01:21:50.240
of people do get quite ill with this.

01:21:50.240 --> 01:21:53.090
And it's not only people over 70.

01:21:53.090 --> 01:21:55.780
Just FYI, there are younger people who

01:21:55.780 --> 01:21:56.670
have gotten quite ill.

01:21:56.670 --> 01:21:58.969
Have been in the ICU and even died.

01:21:58.969 --> 01:22:01.845
And one of the factors, it's not just

01:22:01.845 --> 01:22:03.804
a property of the virus.

01:22:03.804 --> 01:22:05.958
It's a property of the healthcare system

01:22:05.958 --> 01:22:10.830
and its ability to cope with
the number of sick people.

01:22:10.830 --> 01:22:12.990
If you have that number of sick people who

01:22:12.990 --> 01:22:14.563
need that intensive care.

01:22:15.490 --> 01:22:18.009
That matches or is less than
the number of ventilators

01:22:18.009 --> 01:22:20.252
and the number of doctors available.

01:22:20.252 --> 01:22:23.170
The mortality rate is gonna be much lower

01:22:23.170 --> 01:22:25.040
and people are gonna do much better.

01:22:25.040 --> 01:22:28.610
So it's not necessarily
just intrinsic to the virus,

01:22:28.610 --> 01:22:30.100
what the mortality rate is.

01:22:30.100 --> 01:22:33.200
It's how fast the virus
spreads and how long

01:22:33.200 --> 01:22:36.833
the healthcare system has to
build up to deal with this.

01:22:37.800 --> 01:22:40.620
So, in the epicenter of
this epidemic in China.

01:22:40.620 --> 01:22:44.410
That happened so fast
everything, it was overwhelmed.

01:22:44.410 --> 01:22:48.360
Whereas in other places the
social distancing measures

01:22:48.360 --> 01:22:51.270
that are being proposed.

01:22:51.270 --> 01:22:54.160
The point is to slow that down.

01:22:54.160 --> 01:22:56.450
To stay below the threshold of what

01:22:56.450 --> 01:22:57.770
the healthcare system can handle.

01:22:57.770 --> 01:22:59.760
And that's gonna make a huge difference

01:22:59.760 --> 01:23:01.243
in the outcomes that people have

01:23:01.243 --> 01:23:03.733
and the mortality rate of the virus.

01:23:06.950 --> 01:23:07.783
- Thanks Ellen.

01:23:09.350 --> 01:23:13.073
- I just add to both of those
statements by Sten and Ellen.

01:23:16.270 --> 01:23:18.930
Although MERS and SARS were not long ago.

01:23:18.930 --> 01:23:22.620
Just the exponential growth in our travel

01:23:22.620 --> 01:23:26.180
is really, I think, year by year going to

01:23:26.180 --> 01:23:28.830
increase the risk of spread to every

01:23:28.830 --> 01:23:31.340
remote corner of the world.

01:23:31.340 --> 01:23:34.020
And I think we can also
view this really in

01:23:34.020 --> 01:23:36.640
a larger global context.

01:23:36.640 --> 01:23:39.220
I think as Ellen was saying,
I was on the call earlier

01:23:39.220 --> 01:23:43.700
with collaborators in an
African country where I worked.

01:23:43.700 --> 01:23:47.883
They have five ventilators
in the capital city, period.

01:23:48.840 --> 01:23:52.200
So, I think we have a
larger global responsibility

01:23:52.200 --> 01:23:53.970
to limit this epidemic.

01:23:53.970 --> 01:23:57.233
Because places that don't
have the resources like we do.

01:23:57.233 --> 01:24:00.680
They're gonna be really
potentially devastated

01:24:00.680 --> 01:24:02.380
by the spread of the virus.

01:24:02.380 --> 01:24:05.341
So, I think there's
another aspect to this.

01:24:05.341 --> 01:24:08.420
And as Ellen said, the experience in China

01:24:08.420 --> 01:24:11.370
shows that areas where there was not a lot

01:24:11.370 --> 01:24:12.670
of great infrastructure.

01:24:12.670 --> 01:24:16.325
Mortality, again, was
really high and that was

01:24:16.325 --> 01:24:21.325
as good an example of that
impact we've seen so far.

01:24:22.170 --> 01:24:25.930
Africa's hopefully not
going to reach the point

01:24:25.930 --> 01:24:30.690
where Europe and the rest
of the world has reached.

01:24:30.690 --> 01:24:32.903
But we just don't know that at this point.

01:24:33.760 --> 01:24:38.760
And I think we need to take
this incredibly seriously.

01:24:39.850 --> 01:24:42.530
- Thanks, so we have a few more minutes

01:24:42.530 --> 01:24:45.027
and a few more questions
I think we can go through.

01:24:45.027 --> 01:24:47.870
The question from a Grace
Parisi in Brooklyn New York.

01:24:47.870 --> 01:24:50.950
She says my son's college
has suspended classes

01:24:50.950 --> 01:24:52.640
and asking everyone to leave.

01:24:52.640 --> 01:24:54.980
If there's a shelter in place
policy for New York City

01:24:54.980 --> 01:24:57.460
will I be able to pick
my son up at the airport?

01:24:57.460 --> 01:24:59.770
The question I think can
broadly be abstracted

01:24:59.770 --> 01:25:01.750
to think about how do
you understand all these

01:25:01.750 --> 01:25:03.760
public health regulations
that are coming down?

01:25:03.760 --> 01:25:05.965
They're telling us we can't
do this and we can't do that.

01:25:05.965 --> 01:25:10.965
What are our obligations and what are?

01:25:13.110 --> 01:25:15.370
Can we get in trouble
for doing certain things

01:25:15.370 --> 01:25:16.980
like picking up our
children at the airport

01:25:16.980 --> 01:25:18.480
or things like that?

01:25:18.480 --> 01:25:20.003
Anybody want to jump in?

01:25:22.510 --> 01:25:24.583
Amy, Amy then Paul.

01:25:26.110 --> 01:25:27.633
- Well, you know in general.

01:25:29.560 --> 01:25:31.340
If you look at some of
the strongest measures

01:25:31.340 --> 01:25:32.173
that have been taken.

01:25:32.173 --> 01:25:34.330
Like the measures in San Francisco

01:25:34.330 --> 01:25:37.970
where there's a shelter
in place, order in place.

01:25:37.970 --> 01:25:41.930
They have exceptions for
essential and necessary activity.

01:25:41.930 --> 01:25:46.370
And so there are exceptions
for necessary work,

01:25:46.370 --> 01:25:49.110
necessary travel with
respect to healthcare.

01:25:49.110 --> 01:25:50.750
And broadly speaking I think what people

01:25:50.750 --> 01:25:52.880
who are writing those orders
are trying to accomplish

01:25:52.880 --> 01:25:54.890
is communicating the
message that everybody

01:25:54.890 --> 01:25:58.540
needs to socially distance
and take this seriously.

01:25:58.540 --> 01:26:01.530
But not to prevent them from doing things

01:26:01.530 --> 01:26:02.780
that are really necessary to be able to

01:26:02.780 --> 01:26:04.410
take care of their families.

01:26:04.410 --> 01:26:08.468
And so, you have to look at any
specific order that came out

01:26:08.468 --> 01:26:13.468
to see how it would impact any
particular place or activity.

01:26:13.760 --> 01:26:18.760
But, my expectation would be
that these are gonna be written

01:26:18.813 --> 01:26:21.240
with the intent and
enforced with the intent

01:26:21.240 --> 01:26:22.850
to allow them to take
care of their families

01:26:22.850 --> 01:26:25.250
and take care of what's necessary to do.

01:26:25.250 --> 01:26:28.190
And they do have
flexibility built into them

01:26:28.190 --> 01:26:29.440
for essential activities.

01:26:29.440 --> 01:26:33.030
And so, I think that hopefully that will

01:26:33.030 --> 01:26:35.940
be the case with ones
going forward as well.

01:26:35.940 --> 01:26:37.370
- Thank you.

01:26:37.370 --> 01:26:39.113
Paul, do you want to weigh in?

01:26:40.170 --> 01:26:44.920
- Just to say that the
principle of doing the things

01:26:44.920 --> 01:26:48.630
that you have to do is to
them as safely as possible.

01:26:48.630 --> 01:26:52.260
And with the social
distancing measures in place

01:26:52.260 --> 01:26:54.010
that are practical to take.

01:26:54.010 --> 01:26:57.470
So for example, picking up
your son at the airport.

01:26:57.470 --> 01:26:59.070
It makes more sense to do that in a way

01:26:59.070 --> 01:27:03.840
that you can get him
home without the crowd

01:27:03.840 --> 01:27:06.630
of public transportation
if it were possible.

01:27:06.630 --> 01:27:09.330
If it's not possible,
then you have to do that.

01:27:09.330 --> 01:27:12.030
We need to do the essential
things that we need to do.

01:27:12.030 --> 01:27:14.560
But minimize the number
of times that we have

01:27:14.560 --> 01:27:18.090
unnecessary exposures or
nonessential activities

01:27:18.090 --> 01:27:20.270
outside the home.

01:27:20.270 --> 01:27:23.720
- So, a little bit of time for
maybe one or two questions.

01:27:23.720 --> 01:27:26.750
This is from Kyle Trantina who's in the

01:27:26.750 --> 01:27:29.260
Yale Systems Biology Institute.

01:27:29.260 --> 01:27:31.130
I know several post docs and grad students

01:27:31.130 --> 01:27:33.050
who intend on doing nonessential research

01:27:33.050 --> 01:27:35.925
throughout the shutdown and I'm concerned.

01:27:35.925 --> 01:27:40.200
There is a notice from the
President of the university

01:27:40.200 --> 01:27:41.843
about nonessential research.

01:27:44.200 --> 01:27:45.950
A bunch of different post
doc and grad students

01:27:45.950 --> 01:27:47.500
are worried about having
to go into the lab

01:27:47.500 --> 01:27:50.390
either by encouragement by their PI

01:27:50.390 --> 01:27:53.843
or on their own sort of
willingness to do that.

01:27:54.872 --> 01:27:56.850
What is going on in terms of research

01:27:57.700 --> 01:28:02.700
that's nonessential at
the university right now?

01:28:08.870 --> 01:28:10.063
Ellen and then Sten.

01:28:14.444 --> 01:28:16.130
- Sten may know more than I do about this.

01:28:16.130 --> 01:28:20.090
But I guess as a researcher,
the head of a research lab.

01:28:20.090 --> 01:28:22.820
Actually, these policies
are evolving very fast.

01:28:22.820 --> 01:28:25.940
It seems like every half
day we have a new guideline.

01:28:25.940 --> 01:28:29.160
But I think that the way
that things are going is

01:28:29.160 --> 01:28:31.390
to shutdown nonessential research.

01:28:31.390 --> 01:28:35.260
There are certain things to
maintain the infrastructure.

01:28:35.260 --> 01:28:37.790
Such as, you know.

01:28:37.790 --> 01:28:41.140
Crucial things for research like stored

01:28:41.140 --> 01:28:43.200
very important samples
and things like that

01:28:43.200 --> 01:28:45.450
which we'll try to maintain
through this hiatus

01:28:45.450 --> 01:28:48.160
to not destroy those resources.

01:28:48.160 --> 01:28:51.630
But I think everyday
we're seeing more and more

01:28:52.690 --> 01:28:55.090
from the administration of the idea that

01:28:55.090 --> 01:28:56.830
nonessential research.

01:28:56.830 --> 01:29:00.250
Which is research that's
not directly related

01:29:00.250 --> 01:29:04.150
to trying to curve this
epidemic is be curtailed.

01:29:04.150 --> 01:29:07.399
Of course, as a research
institution one of our

01:29:07.399 --> 01:29:11.089
reasons for being is to be
able to apply our knowledge

01:29:11.089 --> 01:29:13.890
to try to help in situations like this.

01:29:13.890 --> 01:29:16.790
So, in that case we don't want
to shut those efforts down.

01:29:18.340 --> 01:29:20.440
- Ellen got it exactly right.

01:29:20.440 --> 01:29:23.170
I'll just add one thing
and that is animals.

01:29:23.170 --> 01:29:25.270
The animals have to be taken care of.

01:29:25.270 --> 01:29:27.132
The insectarium has to be maintained.

01:29:27.132 --> 01:29:29.193
So, that's an essential function.

01:29:30.550 --> 01:29:31.770
- [Gregg] Okay.

01:29:31.770 --> 01:29:35.066
- I would just add in addition I think

01:29:35.066 --> 01:29:37.900
there are folks involved
in clinical trials

01:29:37.900 --> 01:29:41.990
at the university that are being followed.

01:29:41.990 --> 01:29:44.580
That are also in experimental therapies.

01:29:44.580 --> 01:29:46.100
That is not being shutdown.

01:29:46.100 --> 01:29:48.780
So, just want to reassure the public that

01:29:48.780 --> 01:29:51.920
those are considered essential of course.

01:29:51.920 --> 01:29:55.743
So, those distinctions are being made.

01:29:57.860 --> 01:30:00.790
- So, I think we have time
for one more question.

01:30:00.790 --> 01:30:03.700
It's from Risa Sodi
who's an Assistant Dean

01:30:03.700 --> 01:30:07.230
of Academic Affairs in the
Yale College Dean's Office.

01:30:07.230 --> 01:30:09.790
She's asking about is
it safe to play tennis

01:30:09.790 --> 01:30:11.900
or pickleball, which I don't
even know what this is,

01:30:11.900 --> 01:30:14.833
outdoors in light of the
guidelines for social distancing.

01:30:15.790 --> 01:30:17.680
I think the question is
what kind of an exercise

01:30:17.680 --> 01:30:20.073
can people get safely during a pandemic?

01:30:24.159 --> 01:30:24.992
Sten.

01:30:26.630 --> 01:30:29.170
- There are sports that are contact sports

01:30:29.170 --> 01:30:33.400
that are very close quarters.

01:30:33.400 --> 01:30:38.220
The extremes would be things
like football and rugby

01:30:38.220 --> 01:30:41.420
but also soccer, volleyball.

01:30:41.420 --> 01:30:43.010
These are not advisable.

01:30:43.010 --> 01:30:45.000
This is a great opportunity for you to

01:30:45.000 --> 01:30:47.050
breathe on each other,
to cough on each other,

01:30:47.050 --> 01:30:49.458
to spit on each other
accidentally of course.

01:30:49.458 --> 01:30:54.458
And that's why the NBA
has suspended its season.

01:30:56.230 --> 01:30:59.410
Now, the tennis players have
also suspended their season

01:30:59.410 --> 01:31:02.300
but it's not so much
because one tennis player

01:31:02.300 --> 01:31:03.940
is going to infect the other.

01:31:03.940 --> 01:31:05.760
It's because there are large crowds

01:31:05.760 --> 01:31:08.510
that go to tennis matches
and watch these matches

01:31:08.510 --> 01:31:10.490
and mingle and socialize.

01:31:10.490 --> 01:31:15.010
So, in theory the further
apart you are with exercise.

01:31:15.010 --> 01:31:17.470
For example, I'm still going running

01:31:17.470 --> 01:31:20.103
and I'm giving wide brief to anyone that I

01:31:20.103 --> 01:31:22.380
have to pass on my run.

01:31:22.380 --> 01:31:27.380
And that would be still social distancing.

01:31:27.630 --> 01:31:29.900
So, one has to use I think common sense.

01:31:29.900 --> 01:31:32.380
The specifics of tennis and pickleball.

01:31:32.380 --> 01:31:36.150
The reality is you might go
and chat with your partner

01:31:36.150 --> 01:31:40.540
and be close to that partner,
that would be the risk.

01:31:40.540 --> 01:31:43.220
Not being at the other
end of the tennis courts.

01:31:43.220 --> 01:31:47.390
So, I think one has to
really think this through

01:31:47.390 --> 01:31:49.210
and be very cautious.

01:31:49.210 --> 01:31:50.180
- [Gregg] Okay.

01:31:50.180 --> 01:31:51.391
- [Sanil] I think these are--

01:31:51.391 --> 01:31:52.439
- [Gregg] Ann, go ahead.

01:31:52.439 --> 01:31:56.370
- I think it's worth
noting that to maintain

01:31:56.370 --> 01:32:01.370
a healthy lifestyle with
exercise and good diet.

01:32:01.540 --> 01:32:03.940
Being able to sleep well and taking care

01:32:03.940 --> 01:32:05.060
of your mental health.

01:32:05.060 --> 01:32:07.550
And reducing the stress
level will all help

01:32:07.550 --> 01:32:08.920
with building your immunity.

01:32:08.920 --> 01:32:11.909
And so that's really
important to reduce the risk

01:32:11.909 --> 01:32:14.940
of getting the infection.

01:32:14.940 --> 01:32:15.981
- [Gregg] Thank you.

01:32:15.981 --> 01:32:17.770
- I think it's a really important point.

01:32:17.770 --> 01:32:21.690
I do very much worry
about public's ability to,

01:32:21.690 --> 01:32:22.920
you know, for the elderly.

01:32:22.920 --> 01:32:25.370
For many folks, it's really
the way that they get out

01:32:25.370 --> 01:32:28.040
is getting to the gym or getting outside.

01:32:28.040 --> 01:32:31.140
And folks should still have their kids

01:32:31.140 --> 01:32:32.543
playing in the backyard.

01:32:33.420 --> 01:32:35.930
Maybe not in large groups or not

01:32:35.930 --> 01:32:37.180
with the neighbors necessarily.

01:32:37.180 --> 01:32:42.180
But some of this network
theory that Dr. Vermund,

01:32:42.970 --> 01:32:44.600
Dr. Vermund talked about.

01:32:44.600 --> 01:32:46.870
We might have to do
more collective thinking

01:32:46.870 --> 01:32:48.560
about how to make this happen.

01:32:48.560 --> 01:32:50.640
There's also challenges in the inner city

01:32:50.640 --> 01:32:53.510
where folks don't have
the backyard necessarily.

01:32:53.510 --> 01:32:55.410
I think we have to put our heads together

01:32:55.410 --> 01:32:57.230
and think about ways that we can

01:32:58.220 --> 01:33:01.018
let individuals get out
there and get some exercise.

01:33:01.018 --> 01:33:05.430
Or relief, mental
anxiety and I think being

01:33:05.430 --> 01:33:09.280
in front of the TV like we are for

01:33:09.280 --> 01:33:11.540
a lot of the time is not healthy.

01:33:11.540 --> 01:33:14.980
So, maybe for the person who asked about

01:33:14.980 --> 01:33:17.460
what you can do as a youngster is maybe

01:33:17.460 --> 01:33:20.820
help us brainstorm about ways that we can

01:33:21.866 --> 01:33:24.390
have people get out a little bit more

01:33:24.390 --> 01:33:27.390
yet still maintain social distancing.

01:33:27.390 --> 01:33:28.250
- Thanks Sanil.

01:33:28.250 --> 01:33:29.837
I want to thank all our panelist for

01:33:29.837 --> 01:33:31.720
being with us this evening from their

01:33:31.720 --> 01:33:33.900
various homes and
apartments across the city.

01:33:33.900 --> 01:33:38.900
Stay safe, stay healthy and we may have

01:33:39.000 --> 01:33:40.430
another one of these down the line.

01:33:40.430 --> 01:33:41.930
But hopefully you learned a lot tonight

01:33:41.930 --> 01:33:44.630
from our wonderful panelist,
thank you very much.

01:33:44.630 --> 01:33:45.630
Goodnight everybody.

01:33:46.643 --> 01:33:49.393
(music)

